window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"][9]={"university_computer_science_en:M9-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M9-L1 – User interfaces, accessibility and software ergonomics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor’s: Elective and practical areas<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor (elective / application)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Optional Bachelor elective topic\n(M9-L1)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading path:<\/strong> M1-L4 (software engineering), M1-L7\n(further areas of computer science), M4-L3 (security and data\nprotection) and M6-L4 (AI agents) are helpful. However, this chapter can\nbe understood on its own. It requires neither programming nor the\ninstallation of a design tool. For further statistical study, the\nrelevant lessons in the mathematics course are School (German-language)\nM2-L16 and University M1-L7.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why the user interface is part of the system<\/h2>\n<p>An application can be programmed correctly in technical terms and\nstill fail to fulfil its purpose. A word processor that reliably saves\ndocuments is of little help if its users cannot find the Save button. An\nonline service may scale extremely well and still lose orders because\nits forms are incomprehensible. An AI assistant may produce a good\nanswer and nevertheless cause harm if the interface presents an\nassumption as a verified fact.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>user interface<\/strong> (UI) denotes the visible, audible\nor otherwise perceptible means through which people interact with a\nsystem: buttons, menus, text fields, messages, voice input or physical\ncontrols. <strong>User experience<\/strong> (UX) denotes the broader\nexperience of use: expectations, understanding, success, effort and\nfeelings before, during and after use. An aesthetically pleasing UI does\nnot guarantee good UX; conversely, a simple interface can be\nexceptionally reliable to use.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Human–computer interaction<\/strong> (HCI) investigates the\ninterplay of people, tasks and technology. <strong>Usability<\/strong>\ndescribes how well defined groups of people achieve their goals\neffectively, efficiently and satisfactorily in a particular context of\nuse. <strong>Accessibility<\/strong> additionally asks whether people\nwith different physical, sensory, cognitive or situational circumstances\nreceive equal access to the functions. <strong>Software\nergonomics<\/strong> investigates how work with software can be designed\nto suit people and their tasks.<\/p>\n<p>These levels can be viewed separately:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Person with a goal and individual circumstances\n       |\n       v\nInterface: navigation, input, feedback\n       |\n       v\nApplication logic: rules, permissions, processes\n       |\n       v\nDatabase, services, external systems\n       |\n       v\nResult + comprehensible feedback to the person<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An improvement to the interface must not override the business logic.\nFor example, a greyed-out menu item is <strong>not<\/strong> a substitute\nfor a server-side access check. Likewise, a polite notice is not secure\ndata deletion. Good design makes rules understandable; actually\nenforcing them remains the task of the system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From user goal to interaction sequence<\/h2>\n<p>A poor starting question is: ‘Which buttons should we build?’ A\nbetter one is: ‘What does the person want to achieve, under what\ncircumstances, and how will they recognise success?’ The difference\nseems small but changes the entire design.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose someone wants to find all photographs from a particular year\nin a media library, select three of them and save them in a collection.\nThe domain task consists of <em>searching, checking, selecting and\nsaving<\/em>. A particular sidebar, icon colour or card view is not\ninitially necessary for this. Another person may want to achieve the\nsame goal with a keyboard, a screen reader or large text.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Task analysis<\/strong> describes the path from the starting\nstate to the desired result. Four questions help: what does the person\nalready know? What information is missing? What step do they expect?\nWhat are the consequences of errors? An occasional mistake when sorting\nis usually harmless; accidentally making a private album public can\ncause considerable harm.<\/p>\n<p>A <em>usage scenario<\/em> describes a concrete process. A\n<em>persona<\/em> is a condensed, hypothetical description of a user\ngroup. Personas can structure conversations, but are <strong>not\nempirical evidence<\/strong> of actual needs. They must not replace real\nuser research on the basis of stereotypes. Observed working steps,\ntraceable complaints, support requests and conversations with members of\nthe target group are more informative.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"21-mentale-modelle-und-vorhersagbarkeit\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mental models and\npredictability<\/h3>\n<p>People bring <strong>mental models<\/strong> with them: ideas about\nhow software works. A recycle bin usually means ‘recoverable later’, a\ndownload icon means ‘save file’, and a back arrow means ‘view the\nprevious step’. If the application departs from these expectations, it\nmust explain the difference clearly.<\/p>\n<p>A consistent product uses the same words and symbols for the same\naction. If a button means ‘archive’ in one place and ‘delete’ elsewhere,\nunnecessary errors arise. Consistency also includes system conventions:\nkeyboard shortcuts, Tab navigation, scrolling behaviour and dialogue\nboxes should be as predictable as possible.<\/p>\n<p>In design, an <strong>affordance<\/strong> denotes the perceptible\naction possibility of an element; a clearly drawn switch suggests\ntoggling. A <strong>signifier<\/strong> is a clue to that possibility,\nsuch as a label or visible handle. Both can be misleading: a blue\nunderlined word looks like a link even though it may not be clickable.\nGood interfaces make the intended action apparent instead of forcing\ntheir users to guess.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Orientation, navigation and information architecture<\/h2>\n<p>For small applications, a single page is often sufficient. Larger\nsystems need an <strong>information architecture<\/strong>:\ncomprehensible groups, categories, search paths and hierarchies. Good\nnavigation continually answers three questions: <em>Where am I? What can\nI do here? How do I get back?<\/em><\/p>\n<p>An example using a media library:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Library\n |-- All media\n |     |-- Search and filters\n |     +-- Detail view\n |-- My collections\n |-- Import jobs\n |     +-- Progress and errors\n +-- Settings\n       |-- Data protection\n       +-- Accessibility / display<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Categories should be based on the users’ perspective. ‘Import jobs’\nmay be appropriate for technical users; for a general audience, ‘Add\nfiles’ may be easier to understand. Sometimes an interface needs\ndifferent labels in expert and simple views. Such decisions should be\ntested, not made purely according to taste.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Progressive disclosure<\/strong> means displaying advanced\noptions only when they become relevant. A simple photo import could\ninitially offer only ‘Choose files’ and ‘Start’; technical options such\nas metadata strategy, hash checking or batch size remain in an ‘Advanced\nsettings’ section. Hidden functions must remain discoverable; ‘show\nless’ must not mean ‘withhold important information’.<\/p>\n<p>A search function should explain <strong>what<\/strong> it searches.\n‘Search’ without a clear meaning creates false expectations. Does it\nsearch file names, descriptions or also image content recognised by AI?\nIs there a filter for ‘own collections only’? Hidden filters can cause\nsomeone to incorrectly believe their data has disappeared.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Feedback, errors and safe decisions<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Visibility of system status<\/strong> is one of the usability\nprinciples described by Jakob Nielsen [1]. After an action, the person\nshould understand as far as possible whether it has been accepted, is\nbeing processed, has been completed or has failed. This is especially\nimportant for cloud services: clicking ‘Upload’ may only initiate the\nsubmission of the job; processing and storage take longer.<\/p>\n<p>A good workflow distinguishes the states:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Files selected\n        |\n        v\nUpload in progress ---&gt; Transfer error / retry\n        |\n        v\nUpload completed\n        |\n        v\nProcessing in progress ---&gt; Error report / retry\n        |\n        v\nCollection updated / result visible<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>‘Successful’ should only be displayed when <strong>the specified\noperation<\/strong> has actually succeeded. ‘Job accepted’ and ‘job fully\ncompleted’ are different promises. This relates to M4-L1 and M4-L2:\nqueues, workers and databases may be involved in the background.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"41-verstaendliche-fehlermeldungen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comprehensible error\nmessages<\/h3>\n<p>The message ‘Error 500’ identifies a technical symptom but does\nlittle to help the affected person. More useful is: ‘The changes could\nnot be saved. Your input is still available. Please try again.’ A\ntechnical error code can additionally be provided so that support staff\nor administrators can identify the incident.<\/p>\n<p>An error message should, where possible, state: <em>What happened?\nWhat effect does it have? What can I do?<\/em> For an excessively large\nfile upload, ‘File exceeds 50 MB’ is more helpful than ‘Invalid input’.\nOf course, the stated limit must correspond to the actual server\nconfiguration.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Error prevention<\/strong> is often better than a mere error\nmessage. If someone wants to permanently delete five collections, the\ninterface can summarise their number and names, offer a recovery option\nor specifically warn of irreversible consequences. A dialogue box for\nevery harmless action, on the other hand, becomes disruptive. The more\nserious and difficult to undo the consequence, the more appropriate an\nadditional safeguard is.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>undo function<\/strong> reverses an earlier action as far\nas the domain allows. This is easy for local sorting, but limited for\nemails already sent or external payments. The interface must not promise\n‘Undo’ if the original process has already become irreversible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Accessibility begins with different conditions of use<\/h2>\n<p>Accessibility is often associated too narrowly with blindness or\nwheelchair use. In reality, people may have visual, hearing, mobility or\nlearning impairments, among others; they may rely on voice input,\nmagnification, keyboard control or simplified language. People without\npermanent disabilities also encounter barriers: a broken arm, bright\nsunlight, a poor internet connection, a noisy train compartment or a\nhand currently holding a child.<\/p>\n<p>A functioning accessible interface offers <strong>equivalent\npaths<\/strong> to the same task. It is not enough for the home page to\nbe operable with a keyboard if the crucial Save dialogue can only be\nreached with a mouse. All relevant workflows must be accessible: signing\nin, searching, selecting, paying, resolving errors and finding help.<\/p>\n<p>For websites, the <strong>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines\n(WCAG)<\/strong> of the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) are a central\ntechnical standard. <strong>WCAG 2.2<\/strong> defines verifiable\n<em>success criteria<\/em> at conformance levels A, AA and AAA [2]. AA is\na common target in many projects, but the specific legal requirement\ndepends on the service and jurisdiction. The criteria are minimum\nrequirements for the respective level, not a guarantee that everyone can\nuse everything without difficulty.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"51-wahrnehmbar-bedienbar-verstaendlich-robust\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Perceivable,\noperable, understandable, robust<\/h3>\n<p>The four WCAG principles are:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Perceivable:<\/strong> information and controls can be\nperceived through appropriate sensory channels, for example with text\nalternatives for informative images or captions for relevant\nvideos.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Operable:<\/strong> people can operate interactive elements,\nfor example with a keyboard and a recognisable focus position.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Understandable:<\/strong> content and workflows are\ncomprehensible; controls behave as predictably as possible.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Robust:<\/strong> the technical structure works with\ndifferent browsers and assistive technologies, such as screen\nreaders.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>A fully automated test <strong>cannot comprehensively\ndemonstrate<\/strong> these properties. The W3C explicitly emphasises\nthat tools only assist and that knowledgeable human evaluation is\nnecessary [3]. Wherever possible, testing with different users who are\nactually affected should be added.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concrete accessibility decisions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000073_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Controls in an accessible web interface with visible keyboard focus, textual field labels and a clearly named Save button.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Controls in an accessible web interface\nwith visible keyboard focus, textual field labels and a clearly named\nSave button.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Visible focus, clear labels and keyboard operation are concrete\naccessibility features.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"61-schrift-kontrast-und-farbe\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Typography, contrast and\ncolour<\/h3>\n<p>Text requires sufficient contrast against the background. At level\nAA, WCAG 2.2 generally requires a contrast ratio of <strong>at least\n4.5:1<\/strong> for ordinary text and <strong>at least 3:1<\/strong> for\nlarge text, in each case with defined exceptions [2]. These figures\nrefer to calculated luminance contrast, not perceived colour\ndifferences. Contrast is not a substitute for legible font sizes,\nsufficient line spacing and comprehensible wording.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Example:<\/strong> a light grey ‘Save’ label on a white\nbackground may look elegant but be difficult to recognise. A greater\nluminance difference improves legibility. If an error message can\nadditionally only be recognised by red lettering, a second information\nchannel is missing. Better: a warning symbol, meaningful heading, red\naccent and clear text together.<\/p>\n<p>Colours can aid orientation, but must not carry the\n<strong>only<\/strong> information. A chart with ‘green = passed, red =\nfailed’ should also label the values or use patterns. This helps with\ncolour vision deficiencies, monochrome printing and situations with poor\ncolour reproduction.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"62-tastatur-fokus-und-ziele\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Keyboard, focus and targets<\/h3>\n<p>A person must be able to reach the controls in a sensible order with\na keyboard. <strong>Keyboard focus<\/strong> indicates which element will\nreceive the next key press. If a design removes its outline without\nreplacement, the current position is often no longer recognisable. A\nfixed header must not completely obscure the currently focused button\neither; WCAG 2.2 has specific criteria for this [2, 4].<\/p>\n<p>The WCAG 2.2 success criterion <strong>2.5.8 Target Size\n(Minimum)<\/strong> at level AA generally requires a pointer target to be\nable to <strong>fully contain an axis-aligned square of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24\\times24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nCSS pixels<\/strong> [2]. A circular target with a diameter of only 24\npixels therefore does not automatically meet this size option. An\nimportant exception is <strong>sufficient spacing<\/strong>: for a target\nthat is too small, an imaginary circle with a diameter of 24 pixels is\ncentred on its bounding box; it must not intersect other targets or\ntheir corresponding circles. Other exceptions apply, among other things,\nto equivalent controls, inline links and unmodified browser controls.\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24\\times24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a general ergonomic optimum:<\/strong> the separate WCAG 2.2 level\nAAA specifies\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>44<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>44<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">44\\times44<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nCSS pixels<\/strong> with exceptions (criterion 2.5.5), and generously\nsized controls beyond that may be useful. Actual conformance depends on\nsize, shape, spacing and the specific context of use.<\/p>\n<p>An interface that requires drag and drop generally needs an\nalternative means of operation without dragging for the functions\nconcerned under WCAG 2.2 level AA, unless an exception applies. In a\nsortable list, for example, ‘Move up’ and ‘Move down’ could be\nalternatives.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"63-beschriftung-formulare-und-screenreader\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Labelling, forms and\nscreen readers<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>screen reader<\/strong> reads a structured representation of\nthe user interface aloud or presents it through a Braille display. For\nthis, interactive elements need a comprehensible <strong>role<\/strong>,\nan accessible <strong>name<\/strong> and, where applicable, their current\n<strong>state<\/strong>. A checkbox, for example, should be recognisable\nas a checkbox with a label and state, not as a nameless symbol.<\/p>\n<p>On the web, many native <strong>HTML<\/strong> elements (<em>Hypertext\nMarkup Language<\/em>, the markup language for web pages) have such\nproperties by default: <code>button<\/code>, <code>input<\/code>,\n<code>label<\/code>, headings and lists. The W3C recommendation for the\nuse of <strong>Accessible Rich Internet Applications (ARIA)<\/strong> is,\nin essence, to prefer existing native HTML functionality before\nrecreating it with custom ARIA roles [5]. ARIA adds semantics; it does\nnot automatically create keyboard operation, focus management or correct\nbehaviour.<\/p>\n<p>A mere prompt within a field (<em>placeholder<\/em>) is not a reliable\nsubstitute for a persistent visible label. When errors occur, users\nshould learn <strong>which field<\/strong> is affected and\n<strong>how<\/strong> to correct it. It is also important that the\nmessage is technically associated with the field and accessible to\nassistive technologies [5, 6].<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"64-bewegungen-zeitlimits-und-medien\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Motion, time limits and\nmedia<\/h3>\n<p>Uncontrolled animations can be distracting or affect health.\nAnimations should have a functional justification; where there is\nsubstantial motion, users’ preferences for reducing motion must be taken\ninto account. Audio that starts automatically is often disruptive,\nespecially when using a screen reader or working in an office.<\/p>\n<p>Time limits can become a problem with long forms or slower\ninteraction. Where possible, the application announces an impending\nsession expiry, allows an extension and preserves input securely.\nAuthentication and the security context impose limits: an expired\nsession must not remain open indefinitely purely for convenience.<\/p>\n<p>For videos, captions, audio descriptions or other alternatives are\nrelevant depending on the content and applicable success criterion. A\npurely decorative background video does not require the same textual\ntreatment as a technical tutorial. The specific assessment depends on\nthe purpose of the content [2].<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Responsive design and different devices<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Responsive design<\/strong> means that a layout adapts to\navailable display areas and input conditions. A large desktop table with\n30 columns hardly works on a smartphone. A useful mobile layout might\ninitially show the title, status and most important actions and open\ndetails on request.<\/p>\n<p>Responsive design is more than shrinking every element. It accounts\nfor text enlargement, portrait and landscape orientation, different\ndensities and touch interaction. The order in the source code should\nstill make sense even when columns are displayed visually side by\nside.<\/p>\n<p>A typical error: on large screens, the application displays a side\nmenu; on mobile it is turned into an icon without text that is neither\ndiscoverable nor understandable to screen readers. The space saved is\nthen purchased at the cost of interaction problems. A brief visible\nlabel or a clearly recognisable menu may be better than an abstract\nicon.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Offline and slow-network conditions<\/strong> are also part of\nusability. A web interface should indicate whether an action is still\npending, whether data is cached locally or whether a connection is\nrequired. It must not claim without verification that all changes have\nalready reached the server.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Design systems and technical architecture<\/h2>\n<p>Growing software needs a shared vocabulary for controls. A\n<strong>design system<\/strong> describes, for example, typography,\nspacing, colours, buttons, form fields, status messages and states such\nas ‘disabled’, ‘loading’ or ‘error’. This facilitates consistent use and\nsaves subsequent maintenance work.<\/p>\n<p>A design system, however, is only as good as its actual\nimplementation. A centrally defined ‘accessible dialogue box’ is\nworthless if a special page uses its own inaccessible dialogue. Shared\ncomponents can reduce typical errors, but their specific use must be\ntested: is the dialogue text understandable? Is focus correct? Can the\ndialogue be exited? Is data discarded on closing?<\/p>\n<p>A conceptual architecture:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Shared design system\n  |-- Navigation and headings\n  |-- Forms and error messages\n  |-- Status indicators and dialogues\n  +-- Keyboard / screen reader behaviour\n             |\n             v\n       Domain-specific pages\n  |-- Media import\n  |-- Collections\n  +-- AI analysis\n             |\n             v\n       API and business logic\n  |-- Check permissions\n  |-- Store operations\n  +-- Provide accurate status<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The architecture illustrates a boundary: a design system can promote\nvisual and semantic consistency. <strong>Confidentiality, transaction\ncorrectness or authorisation logic<\/strong> do not arise automatically\nfrom it. These tasks remain with the downstream services (see M4-L3 and\nM3-L5).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measuring usability without reducing people to numbers<\/h2>\n<p>Usability can be investigated systematically. Before a test, it\nshould be established <strong>which task<\/strong> is being tested and\n<strong>which success criterion<\/strong> applies: for example, ‘A person\nfinds an uploaded document and can check the access permissions’. The\ntask should not already reveal which button the person should use.<\/p>\n<p>Important metrics are:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Task success:<\/strong> how many people actually achieve the\ndefined goal?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Completion time:<\/strong> how long do people need for the\nsame task?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Error rate:<\/strong> how frequently do relevant errors\noccur?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Need for assistance:<\/strong> how often must observers\nintervene or provide explanations?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Subjective assessment:<\/strong> how comprehensible,\ndemanding or reliable do people find the workflow?<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A longer completion time is not always worse. If checking important\npermissions requires ten extra seconds but prevents dangerous accidental\nsharing, this can be worthwhile. For people who use assistive\ntechnology, direct time comparisons without context are especially\nlikely to be misleading.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"91-durchgerechnetes-beispiel\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h3>\n<p>A fictional study considers ten people who are to create an image\ncollection with restricted access permissions. In the first design, six\npeople complete the task without assistance; in the revised design, nine\ndo so.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Design A<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Design B<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Participants<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Successful without assistance<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Success rate<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">60 %<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">90 %<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Difference<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">–<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">+30 percentage points<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The success rate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Successful participants<\/mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Participants<\/mtext><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\text{Successful participants}}{\\text{Participants}}\\times100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor A, therefore,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>6<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac6{10}\\times100=60\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor B,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>9<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac9{10}\\times100=90\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe <strong>relative improvement<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>90<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>60<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{90-60}{60}\\times100=50\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhereas the <strong>absolute improvement<\/strong> is 30 percentage\npoints. These two statements are not interchangeable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What follows from this?<\/strong> B was more successful for\nthese ten observed people. <strong>What does not follow?<\/strong> That B\nalways works 50% better for everyone. Ten people are a small sample. The\nprocedure, task order, previous knowledge, selection of participants and\npossible learning effects can influence the result. If the same people\nuse both designs, the second may appear easier simply because of\npractice. Fair comparisons therefore use a justified test design, for\nexample a balanced order.<\/p>\n<p>This is the subject-matter link to statistics and experimental design\nin the mathematics course; <strong>no<\/strong> specific lesson number\nthere is asserted without consulting it.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"92-qualitative-beobachtung-und-quantitative-messung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Qualitative\nobservation and quantitative measurement<\/h3>\n<p>Numbers show <strong>that<\/strong> a task fails, but often not\n<strong>why<\/strong>. Observations and follow-up questions can reveal\nthat people misunderstand the meaning of a word or confuse two different\nsave functions. A good test combines focused observation with simple\nmetrics. Standardised surveys can complement comparisons but should not\nreplace observed tasks.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Heuristic evaluation<\/strong> means that knowledgeable people\nassess an interface using recognised design principles. Nielsen’s ten\nheuristics include, among others, visibility of status, comprehensible\nterms, control/undo, consistency, error prevention and helpful error\nmessages [1]. This is a low-cost initial assessment but not a complete\nevaluation with real users.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Accessibility evaluation is not a single button press<\/h2>\n<p>A useful evaluation process begins before programming: choose clear\ntasks and accessible standard components, think through keyboard\nnavigation and design with comprehensible text. Technical and human\nchecks follow in the running system.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Check structure:<\/strong> are the heading hierarchy, field\nlabels, alternative texts, error associations and keyboard order\ncorrect?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Check presentation:<\/strong> does content remain readable\nand usable with enlarged text, on narrow screens and at high\ncontrast?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Check operation:<\/strong> can all critical paths be\ncompleted with a keyboard, screen reader and, where appropriate, voice\ncontrol?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Check error cases:<\/strong> what happens in the event of\nincorrect input, a lost connection or an interrupted upload?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Test with people:<\/strong> observe people with different\nneeds and take their feedback seriously.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Automated test programs are helpful, for example in discovering\nmissing field names or certain contrast problems. However, they cannot\ndetermine whether a warning is understandable or an alternative action\nreally fulfils the same purpose [3]. <strong>‘Scanner reports zero\nerrors’ therefore does not mean ‘product is fully\naccessible’.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Accessibility can also deteriorate after updates. A new menu\nstructure, changed colours or an additional confirmation dialogue can\nmake previously accessible paths inaccessible again. Recurring checks,\ndocumented responsibilities and opportunities for feedback therefore\nbelong to ongoing operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Legal context in Europe: distinguishing objectives and\nscope<\/h2>\n<p>In European law, the <strong>European Accessibility Act\n(EAA)<\/strong>, Directive (EU) 2019/882, is important for certain\nproducts and services. These include certain digital devices, banking\nservices and <strong>e-commerce<\/strong>, among others. The relevant\nprovisions generally apply from <strong>28 June 2025<\/strong>; Member\nStates transpose the directive into national law [7, 8]. In Germany, the\nAccessibility Strengthening Act\n(<strong>Barrierefreiheitsstärkungsgesetz<\/strong>, BFSG) is\nparticularly relevant in this context.<\/p>\n<p>It does <strong>not<\/strong> follow that every privately operated\nwebsite or every piece of internal software must meet exactly the same\nobligations across the board. The scope depends on the service,\ncontractual situation, provider, transitional rules and statutory\nexceptions. The directive provides exemptions for certain\nmicroenterprises that provide services; these are not equivalent to a\ngeneral exemption for every small organisation [7]. Other provisions may\napply to public bodies.<\/p>\n<p><strong>WCAG 2.2 and the law are not identical either.<\/strong> WCAG\nis a technical guideline, whereas legal obligations arise from the\napplicable legal acts and referenced standards. Anyone operating a real\nshop, banking application or public service must obtain expert\nclarification of the specific scope. This lesson teaches the technical\nconcepts and does not provide individual legal advice.<\/p>\n<p>Regardless of the legal minimum, accessibility often makes economic\nsense: a clear structure reduces queries, errors and support effort. At\nthe same time, it can enable more people to use the service. No specific\nincrease in revenue can be inferred from this without reliable data,\nhowever.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three practical scenarios<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-a-a-local-media-archive-on-a-private-computer\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario\nA: A local media archive on a private computer<\/h3>\n<p>The archive is to manage thousands of photos and videos. Frequent\ntasks include importing, searching, categorising and sorting out\nunwanted items. Technical processing can take place on a powerful local\ncomputer. However, the interface must remain responsive even when\nseveral files are being indexed in the background.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Suitable workflow:<\/strong> during import, the system\nseparately displays the number and type of selected files, transfer\nprogress into the archive, processing still in progress and possible\nerrors. Search enables keyboard operation, readable filters and a clear\nindication if indexing is still incomplete. Before permanent removal,\nthe person can identify whether only a catalogue entry is being deleted\nor the original file is being removed from storage.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Accessibility:<\/strong> previews receive meaningful file\nlabels or, if reliable descriptions exist, additional image\ndescriptions. Automatically generated AI tags are labelled as\nsuggestions, not as guaranteed facts. Selecting many images must not be\npossible only by dragging with the mouse.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Costs and limitations:<\/strong> an elegant interface does not\nreplace backups (see M3-L6). Elaborate animations and large preview\nrenderings can place additional demands on CPU, GPU and RAM. The\nsoftware should reserve computing capacity for the actual archive\ntask.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"scenario-b-an-online-analysis-service-with-customer-accounts\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario\nB: An online analysis service with customer accounts<\/h3>\n<p>A web service accepts documents, analyses them and displays a results\ndashboard. The system uses an API, a database and background workers.\nThe user interface connects these components to customer\nexpectations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Suitable workflow:<\/strong> the customer sees a\ncomprehensible ‘Analysis started’ message, followed by the actual\nprogress status and finally the result with sources or explanations. If\nit fails, it is clear whether a retry may incur costs. A failed payment\nand a failed document analysis are treated as different operations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Accessibility:<\/strong> forms have persistently visible\nlabels and comprehensible error messages and can be reached in a logical\norder with the keyboard. Charts include textual information or tabular\nvalues in addition to coloured areas. Status changes are available in a\nform suitable for screen readers without continually interrupting the\nreading flow for every minor update.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Security and data protection:<\/strong> hiding an Export\nbutton is insufficient to restrict permissions. The server checks\npermissions, logs contain as little unnecessary personal content as\npossible, and analysis results are displayed only to the authorised\naccount. For a commercial service, the actual legal classification\nconcerning accessibility must also be checked.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"scenario-c-an-ai-assistant-with-tool-calls\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scenario C: An AI\nassistant with tool calls<\/h3>\n<p>An assistant is to summarise documents, read a calendar and, after\napproval, possibly create an appointment. The critical question is how\nusers distinguish an <strong>AI suggestion<\/strong>, an <strong>executed\naction<\/strong> and a <strong>confirmed result<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User: &quot;Schedule the appointment for Friday.&quot;\n                 |\n                 v\nAI: interprets request + formulates suggestion\n                 |\n                 v\nInterface displays date, time, participants, access\n                 |\n         Person checks and confirms\n                 |\n                 v\nServer checks permissions and executes action\n                 |\n                 v\nCalendar returns actual success or error message\n                 |\n                 v\nInterface displays confirmed status<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Comprehensibility:<\/strong> the proposed appointment is not\npresented as ‘scheduled’ until the calendar API has confirmed execution.\nUncertain data or outstanding questions remain visible. A misinterpreted\ntime can be corrected before the final entry is made.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Accessibility:<\/strong> the confirmation dialogue works with\na keyboard and a screen reader; information is available as text rather\nthan solely through coloured highlighting. Voice control can complement\nthese methods but must not be the only means of access. Multi-stage\ntasks need clear intermediate statuses and cancellation options.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Security:<\/strong> content from external documents must not\nprovide hidden instructions for bypassing confirmations. Separate\nauthorisation checks, precisely defined tool boundaries and human\napprovals help particularly against <strong>prompt injection<\/strong>\nand unauthorised actions (see M6-L4). Accessibility and security are not\nopposites here: a legible, verifiable confirmation can strengthen both\nobjectives.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When different levels of effort are appropriate<\/h2>\n<p>Not every project needs a large UX team or an extensive design\nsystem. An application used privately on a one-off basis can manage with\na few consistent pages. What matters is that its core tasks work\ncomprehensibly and safely. As soon as many people, recurring business\nprocesses or dangerous erroneous actions are involved, the benefit of\nsystematic usability and accessibility evaluation increases\nconsiderably.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation<\/th>\n<th>Useful focus<\/th>\n<th>Often unnecessary effort<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple local note-taking application<\/td>\n<td>Clear navigation, saving, retrieving<\/td>\n<td>Large-scale design system without multiple products<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public form<\/td>\n<td>Unambiguous fields, keyboard, error handling, data protection<\/td>\n<td>Elaborate animations without purpose<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complex specialist dashboard<\/td>\n<td>Information architecture, filters, status, accessible data\nviews<\/td>\n<td>Displaying all information simultaneously<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI agent with external actions<\/td>\n<td>Transparent suggestions, approvals, result status<\/td>\n<td>Automating decisions without comprehensible oversight<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Maintenance and economics:<\/strong> accessible standard\ncomponents and consistent texts initially require some planning effort\nbut can make subsequent errors and changes easier to handle. A wholly\ncustom interaction system incurs ongoing costs for development, testing,\ndocumentation and maintenance. With a limited budget, well-supported\nstandard techniques are often cheaper and more reliable than newly\ninvented controls. Specific costs, however, require estimates for the\nactual project.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Performance:<\/strong> users experience even a technically\nfast application as slow when they receive no feedback. Conversely, a\nvisible loading indicator can make waiting times easier to understand\nwithout physically shortening them. An animation must never conceal a\nfailed operation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Data protection:<\/strong> usage analytics can help discover\nstumbling blocks. They must, however, be designed to be minimal,\npurpose-bound and transparent. Recording individual keyboard events,\nsensitive form entries or complete chat messages indiscriminately for UX\nevaluation may be disproportionate. Aggregated statistics and voluntary\nfeedback may be sufficient in many cases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Common misconceptions and the actual lessons<\/h2>\n<p><strong>‘Beautiful design is already good UX.’<\/strong> Not\nnecessarily. A visually impressive interface may neglect orientation,\nprotection against errors and accessibility. Design is measured against\nconcrete usage goals, not solely appearance.<\/p>\n<p><strong>‘Accessibility is a special mode for a few people.’<\/strong>\nNo. Accessible structures help many different people; the goal is equal\nparticipation. A special view added later may conceal problems in the\nactual application rather than solving them.<\/p>\n<p><strong>‘With ARIA, any element can be made accessible.’<\/strong>\nARIA describes roles and states, among other things; a custom-built\nbutton still requires keyboard operation, focus management and reliable\nbehaviour. Native components are often the more robust starting point\n[5].<\/p>\n<p><strong>‘An automated scanner has passed everything.’<\/strong>\nScanners can identify relevant problems but cannot automatically assess\nall aspects. Actual usage paths and knowledgeable evaluation remain\nnecessary [3].<\/p>\n<p><strong>‘WCAG 2.2 mandates 24 pixels for every button on every\nplatform.’<\/strong> The stated size belongs to a particular web success\ncriterion at level AA, with exceptions and defined measurement rules. It\nis not a universal operating-system law and is not always a comfortable\nsize [2].<\/p>\n<p><strong>‘A multilingual AI automatically understands all\nusers.’<\/strong> Language models can support understandable text but\nalso produce unclear wording, incorrect translations or unsupported\nclaims. The user interface must represent uncertainty, permit\ncancellation and independently validate critical actions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>‘An interaction restriction can be secured by hiding the\nbutton.’<\/strong> No. An attacker can address the API directly. Security\nrequires server-side checks (see M4-L3).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and subject-matter context<\/h2>\n<p>User interfaces form a link between people, domain tasks and\ntechnical infrastructure. A good interface makes possible actions,\nsystem states and consequences visible. It helps prevent errors,\nprovides safe correction options and avoids excluding anyone through\nunnecessary interaction barriers as far as possible.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The central guiding question:<\/strong> not ‘Which interface\nwould be impressive?’ but ‘Can a real person complete their task\nsuccessfully, comprehensibly and safely under their actual\ncircumstances?’ This question makes navigation, status messages,\ncontrast, keyboard operation, forms, testing methods and software\narchitecture amenable to planning.<\/p>\n<p>M9-L1 builds on M1-L4 (software design), M1-L7 (HCI), M4-L3\n(security/data protection), M4-L2 (status monitoring) and M6-L4 (AI\nagents). For the planning and evaluation of user studies, School\n(German-language) M2-L16 and University M1-L7 in the mathematics course\ndeepen the statistical foundations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Placement in the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This is an <strong>optional Bachelor elective lesson<\/strong>, not an\nadditional main course lesson. Among other connections, it leads to <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1 in the Master core<\/a>. Further\nroutes: <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course-wide reading\npaths<\/a> and <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course index<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Documented source status:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<p>The following specialist and primary sources were cross-checked on\n<strong>04.11.2026<\/strong>. They substantiate the technical and\ndidactic statements; the media archives, measured values and AI\nworkflows mentioned are <strong>specially constructed teaching\nexamples<\/strong>, not documented product investigations.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Nielsen Norman Group (Jakob Nielsen):<\/strong> <em>10\nUsability Heuristics for User Interface Design<\/em>, originally 1994,\nlast reviewed 2024. <a\nhref=\"https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\">https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>World Wide Web Consortium (W3C):<\/strong> <em>Web Content\nAccessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2<\/em>, Recommendation; success\ncriteria concerning contrast, keyboard, focus, target size and input. <a\nhref=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\">https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>W3C Web Accessibility Initiative:<\/strong> <em>Evaluating\nWeb Accessibility Overview<\/em>. <a\nhref=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/\">https://www.w3.org/WAI/test-evaluate/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>W3C WAI:<\/strong> <em>What’s New in WCAG 2.2<\/em>. <a\nhref=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/new-in-22/\">https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/new-in-22/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>W3C:<\/strong> <em>Using ARIA<\/em> and <em>Accessible Rich\nInternet Applications (WAI-ARIA) 1.2<\/em>. <a\nhref=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/using-aria/\">https://www.w3.org/TR/using-aria/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria/\">https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>W3C WAI ARIA Authoring Practices Guide:<\/strong>\n<em>Providing Accessible Names and Descriptions<\/em>; native labels and\ncomprehensible names. <a\nhref=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/practices/names-and-descriptions/\">https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/practices/names-and-descriptions/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>European Commission:<\/strong> <em>European Accessibility Act\n(EAA)<\/em>, Directive (EU) 2019/882. <a\nhref=\"https://commission.europa.eu/strategy-and-policy/policies/justice-and-fundamental-rights/disability/european-accessibility-act-eaa_en\">https://commission.europa.eu/strategy-and-policy/policies/justice-and-fundamental-rights/disability/european-accessibility-act-eaa_en<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>EUR-Lex:<\/strong> <em>Accessibility of products and\nservices<\/em>, summary of scope, timing and exceptions. <a\nhref=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/EN/legal-content/summary/accessibility-of-products-and-services.html\">https://eur-lex.europa.eu/EN/legal-content/summary/accessibility-of-products-and-services.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This lesson conveys technical foundations and general context;\n<strong>it is not an individual legal compliance assessment<\/strong>.\nDetails of legal obligations must be checked against current provisions\nand the particular service.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M9-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M9-L2 – Data analysis, visualisation and statistical thinking<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor’s: Elective and practical areas<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor (elective / application)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Optional Bachelor elective topic\n(M9-L2)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading path:<\/strong> M1-L5 (data and databases), M8-L1\n(discrete structures), M9-L1 (usability) and M5-L3 (machine learning)\nare helpful. However, the lesson can be read on its own. It requires\nneither statistical software, programming nor exercises. In the\nmathematics course, School (German-language) M1-L6 introduces\ndescriptive statistics. School M2-L16 and University M1-L7 explore\nrandom variables, samples and statistical tests in greater depth.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data does not automatically constitute knowledge<\/h2>\n<p>A computer can record millions of rows and still deliver the wrong\nanswer. A website reports 20,000 page views. Does this mean 20,000\npeople? Not necessarily: one person may load ten pages, a program may\naccess the site repeatedly, and some accesses may be missed by the\nmeasurement. The number is technically plausible, but its meaning is\nunclear without a <strong>definition<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Data analysis<\/strong> means systematically examining,\npreparing, summarising and interpreting collected observations to answer\na specific question. <strong>Statistics<\/strong> provides concepts and\nmethods for describing patterns and assessing uncertainty. <strong>Data\nvisualisation<\/strong> makes selected relationships visible. Above all,\n<strong>statistical thinking<\/strong> means asking <em>what<\/em> was\nmeasured, <em>among whom<\/em>, <em>under what conditions<\/em> and\n<em>what conclusion can legitimately be drawn<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p>The entire chain can be understood as an architecture:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Real-world process (person, machine, event)\n                 |\n                 v\nMeasurement / collection / event log\n                 |\n                 v\nRaw data + definitions + time of collection\n                 |\n                 v\nQuality checks and traceable cleaning\n                 |\n                 v\nMetrics / comparisons / statistical models\n                 |\n                 v\nChart + interpretation + uncertainty\n                 |\n                 v\nDecision, observation or new question<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Each transition may introduce errors. If a time zone change is\noverlooked, hourly usage figures may be grouped incorrectly. A\nduplicated data row can suddenly turn one job into two. Graphics\nsoftware can make a tiny difference look dramatic. Data analysis is\ntherefore not a one-off act of creating a chart, but a process that can\nbe checked.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A question determines the analysis<\/h2>\n<p>Every calculation begins with a verifiable question. “How well is the\napplication doing?” is imprecise. “How many of the import jobs started\nin the past seven days ended successfully?” can be checked. The second\nsentence specifies the <em>unit<\/em> (import job), <em>outcome<\/em>\n(successful completion), <em>population<\/em> (jobs started) and\n<em>period<\/em>.<\/p>\n<p>Three questions should remain separate:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Description:<\/strong> How many jobs were there, and how long\ndid they take?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:<\/strong> Are longer runtimes associated with\nfile size or utilisation?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Question of effect:<\/strong> Would an additional machine\nactually shorten the runtime?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The third question requires stronger evidence than the first. Two\nvalues may increase together without one causing the other. For example,\nboth visits to a bathing area and sales of drinks increase on warm days.\nThis does not mean that drink sales cause the number of visitors.<\/p>\n<p>Before an analysis, it should also be clear <em>which decision<\/em>\nit is intended to support. If a report changes no action, a small\noverview may suffice instead of a permanent analytics cluster.\nConversely, decisions with legal or safety implications may require\nseveral assessment methods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What exactly is a data row?<\/h2>\n<p>A table appears unambiguous until we question its <strong>unit of\nobservation<\/strong>. A record may represent a person, order, file,\nsession, device measurement or processing step. Mixing these levels\nproduces incorrect totals.<\/p>\n<p>Consider a fictional media archive:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>File ID<\/th>\n<th>Type<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Size (MiB)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Import duration (s)<\/th>\n<th>Outcome<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>M001<\/td>\n<td>Photo<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td>Successful<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M002<\/td>\n<td>Video<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">240<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">18<\/td>\n<td>Successful<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M003<\/td>\n<td>Photo<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td>Successful<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M004<\/td>\n<td>Audio<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">70<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td>Failed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Here <strong>one row represents one import attempt<\/strong>. If the\nsame file is imported again, a new attempt with its own ID should be\nrecorded. The total number of attempts is not the same as the number of\ndistinct files.<\/p>\n<p>The properties of a row are called <strong>variables<\/strong> or\n<strong>features<\/strong>. <code>Type<\/code> and <code>Outcome<\/code>\nare categorical features; <code>Size<\/code> and\n<code>Import duration<\/code> are quantitative features. An ID is not a\nmeaningful number to average, even if it consists entirely of digits. A\ndate field belongs on a timeline; free text can only be categorised\nafter clear rules have been defined.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Nominal categories<\/strong> have no natural order, such as\nphoto, audio and video. <strong>Ordinal categories<\/strong> have an\norder, such as low, medium and high; however, the distance between them\nis not automatically measurable. Quantitative values permit arithmetic\nstatements if the unit and measurement scale are appropriate.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Metadata<\/strong> describes the data: measurement method,\nunit, source, time of collection, version, calculation and any filters.\nWithout it, a number such as “75” is neither 75 milliseconds nor 75\neuros nor 75 per cent. A good data model records the meaning even if\nother people read the report later.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data quality: checks precede calculations<\/h2>\n<p>Typical problems include duplicated rows, missing values,\ninconsistent categories, invalid timestamps, mixed units and\nobservations that do not belong to the specified population.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Data cleaning<\/strong> is the documented identification and\nhandling of such problems. It should not secretly “smooth out”\nreality:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>An import duration of <code>-3 seconds<\/code> is physically\nimplausible for a single completed process. It may indicate a clock\nerror or an incorrect calculation.<\/li>\n<li>A missing price is <strong>not<\/strong> automatically a price of\nzero euros. <code>NULL<\/code>, “unknown” and <code>0<\/code> describe\ndifferent states.<\/li>\n<li>The values <code>DE<\/code>, <code>Deutschland<\/code> and\n<code>Germany<\/code> may be assigned to the same country category; the\ndecision belongs in a mapping table.<\/li>\n<li>Two identical rows may represent genuinely repeated events or a log\nthat has been read more than once. Deletion is only appropriate once the\n<strong>event ID<\/strong> or collection procedure has been\nclarified.<\/li>\n<li>A surprisingly large value is not automatically a measurement error.\nPerhaps a rare large file really did occur.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Missing information may occur randomly, but it may also be\nconcentrated systematically in particular groups. If users with slow\nconnections frequently abandon their uploads and precisely those\nattempts are absent from the success report, average performance will be\nportrayed too positively. <strong>Imputation<\/strong> means replacing\nmissing values with justified estimates. This is a modelling assumption\nand should remain visible rather than being confused with an actual\nmeasurement.<\/p>\n<p>A practical procedure is to retain raw data unchanged, log checking\nsteps, generate a separate cleaned table and flag questionable cases.\nAnyone who later makes a different cleaning decision can use this to\nreproduce the report.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Descriptive statistics: what does a distribution look\nlike?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Descriptive statistics<\/strong> describes existing data. Its\ntools include frequencies, proportions, means, the median, dispersion,\nquantiles and graphical representations. Description does not yet\njustify drawing conclusions from one group about all people or future\nevents.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"51-ein-vollstaendig-gerechnetes-beispiel\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A fully worked\nexample<\/h3>\n<p>In a fictional example, seven import jobs take the following numbers\nof seconds:<\/p>\n<p><code>2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 7, 18<\/code><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>arithmetic mean<\/strong> is the sum divided by the number\nof observations:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>7<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>42<\/mn><mn>7<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{2+3+3+4+5+7+18}{7}=\\frac{42}{7}=6\\,\\text{seconds}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>median<\/strong> is the middle value of the sorted list.\nHere the fourth position contains <code>4<\/code>, so <strong>median = 4\nseconds<\/strong>. The <strong>mode<\/strong> is the most frequently\noccurring value: <code>3 seconds<\/code> (twice).<\/p>\n<p>Why do the mean and median differ? The job taking 18 seconds pulls\nthe mean upwards. If that value were excluded for a <em>separate<\/em>\ncomparison, the mean of the remaining six jobs would be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>24<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{24}{6}=4\\,\\text{seconds}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSimply deleting that job would not be justified, however. It may be a\nrare, genuine heavy-load case that the operator particularly needs to\nunderstand.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>range<\/strong> is the largest minus the smallest value:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>18<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">18-2=16\\,\\text{seconds}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is strongly affected by extreme values. <strong>Quartiles<\/strong>\ndivide sorted values into sections. Under the convention explicitly used\nhere, the median is excluded from the quartile calculation when the\nnumber of values is odd: the lower half <code>2, 3, 3<\/code> gives\n<strong>Q1 = 3<\/strong>, and the upper half <code>5, 7, 18<\/code> gives\n<strong>Q3 = 7<\/strong>. The <strong>interquartile range<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{IQR}=7-3=4\\,\\text{seconds}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOther statistical programs sometimes use different quantile conventions,\nso threshold values may differ slightly.<\/p>\n<p>According to a commonly used <em>rule of thumb<\/em>, an upper outlier\nthreshold is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_3+1{,}5\\times\\mathrm{IQR}=7+6=13\\,\\text{seconds}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe value 18 would therefore <strong>deserve flagging<\/strong>, but\nwould not automatically be wrong. The rule is an aid to investigation,\nnot an instruction to delete data.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"52-standardabweichung-und-streuung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Standard deviation and\ndispersion<\/h3>\n<p>The <strong>variance<\/strong> averages squared distances from the\nmean; the <strong>standard deviation<\/strong> is its square root. It\ntherefore has the unit seconds again. In our complete list of seven\nvalues, the mean is 6. The squared distances sum to:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>144<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>184<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16+9+9+4+1+1+144=184\\,\\text{seconds}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>If the seven jobs are treated as <strong>the entire population under\nconsideration<\/strong>, the population variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>184<\/mn><mn>7<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>26<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>29<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{184}{7}\\approx26{,}29\\,\\text{seconds}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the population standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx5{,}13\\,\\text{seconds}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the same list were analysed as a <strong>sample<\/strong> of a larger\npopulation, the simple unbiased sample variance under the usual\nassumptions would use the denominator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n - 1 = 6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>67<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx30{,}67\\,\\text{seconds}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith a sample standard deviation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>54<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx5{,}54\\,\\text{seconds}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The two calculations answer different questions; the denominators\nmust not be exchanged without notice. A standard deviation alone does\nnot indicate whether a distribution is symmetric or contains a few\nextreme cases. The median, quantiles and a distribution chart therefore\noften complement the mean usefully [1].<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Percentages, reference quantities and meaningful\nmetrics<\/h2>\n<p>A percentage must always make its <strong>denominator<\/strong>\napparent. Out of 100 imports started, 90 succeed: a <strong>90% success\nrate<\/strong>. However, if 20 attempts are still running, the statement\n“90 out of 100 ultimately successful” would be premature. One could\nreport the status as <em>90 successful, 10 failed, 20 pending<\/em> out\nof a total of 120 starts, or deliberately examine the completed jobs\nseparately.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>rate<\/strong> relates events to a unit of time or quantity:\nfor example, 30 import failures per 10,000 jobs, rather than simply “30\nfailures”. For time series, the measurement window must be included: 100\njobs in an hour represent a different load from 100 jobs in a day.<\/p>\n<p>Percentages and <strong>percentage points<\/strong> are not the same.\nIf a success rate rises from 60 to 75 per cent, the absolute increase is\n<strong>15 percentage points<\/strong>. Relative to the initial rate,\nthis is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>75<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>60<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{75-60}{60}=25\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth descriptions are mathematically correct; however, simply stating\n“25% better” may easily sound exaggerated if the baseline is\nunclear.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"61-das-gewichtete-mittel\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The weighted mean<\/h3>\n<p>An analytics report shows two job classes: 90 small jobs take an\naverage of 1 second, and 10 large jobs take an average of 10 seconds.\nSimply averaging the class means gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{1+10}{2}=5{,}5\\,\\text{seconds}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich is wrong for the <strong>average duration of a randomly selected\njob<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>weighted mean<\/strong> takes the different group sizes\ninto account:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>90<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>90<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>190<\/mn><mn>100<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{90\\times1+10\\times10}{90+10}=\\frac{190}{100}=1{,}9\\,\\text{seconds}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The number <code>5,5<\/code> would at most be the average <em>of two\nclass means with equal class weighting<\/em> – a different question.\nTables, charts and reports must therefore always disclose whether the\nweights are based on people, jobs, files or groups.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Frequencies, distributions and probabilities<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>frequency distribution<\/strong> counts how often values or\ncategories occur. Discrete categories can be counted directly; where\nmany distinct numerical values exist, ranges are formed. The boundaries\nmust be unambiguously specified, for example\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0 \\le  t &lt; 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2 \\le  t &lt; 5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5 \\le  t &lt; 10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t \\ge  10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nseconds<\/strong>. Under this convention, a job taking exactly 2 seconds\nbelongs to the second class and one taking exactly 10 seconds belongs to\nthe last. The intervals do not overlap and leave no gaps in the\nnon-negative measurement range. Other class boundaries are possible;\ntheir width changes the impression of the distribution.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Probability<\/strong> deals with a model of possible events,\nwhile <strong>relative frequency<\/strong> describes the observed\nproportion. If nine out of ten files were imported successfully, the\nobserved success rate is 90%. This does not mean the next import has\nexactly a 90% probability of success. For that, conditions would need to\nbe sufficiently stable and the sample informative.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Conditional probability<\/strong> means: how likely is an\nevent <em>if<\/em> an additional condition holds? For example, the\nfailure rate for videos may be higher than for photos. An overall rate\nconceals these differences. Conversely, small groups must be interpreted\ncautiously: one failure in two jobs gives 50%, whereas the same single\nfailure in 2,000 jobs would give only 0.05%.<\/p>\n<p>When assessing rare events, the <strong>base rate<\/strong> plays an\nimportant role. A fictional classifier is intended to identify 100\nsuspicious cases out of 10,000 events. It identifies 90 of the 100\ngenuinely suspicious cases (90% recall) and incorrectly flags 5% of the\n9,900 non-suspicious cases: <strong>495 false alarms<\/strong>. It\nreports a total of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>90<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>495<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>585<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90 + 495 = 585<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuspicious cases. Only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>90<\/mn><mn>585<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>38<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{90}{585}\\approx15{,}38\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof these are genuinely suspicious. Although recall sounds high, a\npositive result is therefore usually a false alarm.\n<strong>Precision<\/strong> and <strong>recall<\/strong> answer different\nquestions – a connection to M5-L3.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Population, sample and selection bias<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>population<\/strong> is the set about which we want to\nmake a statement: for instance, all customers, all requests this month\nor all videos to be processed in future. A <strong>sample<\/strong> is\nthe observed subset. Not every sample represents the population.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>random sample<\/strong> uses a documented selection rule\nwith known selection probabilities; under appropriate assumptions, this\npermits estimates of statistical error. A voluntary online survey, by\ncontrast, may attract particularly satisfied or particularly\ndissatisfied people. More responses do not automatically eliminate this\n<strong>selection bias<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>A distinction is made between <strong>sampling errors<\/strong>,\ndifferences arising from random selection, and <strong>non-sampling\nerrors<\/strong>, such as incorrect questions, measurement errors,\nmissing responses, incomplete coverage or erroneous processing. Even a\ncensus may contain non-sampling errors [2].<\/p>\n<p>An example: of 100 randomly selected, independent cases, 60 are\nsuccessful. The estimated proportion is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p = 0{,}60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder the assumptions of a simple binomial model, the estimated\n<strong>standard error<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>100<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>049<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\frac{0{,}6\\times0{,}4}{100}}\\approx0{,}049<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor approximately 4.9 percentage points. A rough normal approximation for\na 95% <strong>confidence interval<\/strong> gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><mo>±<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>96<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">percentage points<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\,\\%\\pm1{,}96\\times4{,}9\\,\\text{percentage points}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor approximately <strong>50.4% to 69.6%<\/strong>. The exact limits\ndepend on the chosen method.<\/p>\n<p>This calculation is <strong>not a guarantee<\/strong> that the true\nproportion lies in this particular computed interval. In simplified\nterms, over very many repetitions of the same random sampling procedure,\napproximately 95% of the intervals constructed by this method would\ncover the fixed true parameter, provided the assumptions hold. The\ninterval does not remedy systematic selection errors or poor measurement\nmethods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Association is not causation<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Correlation<\/strong> describes how two quantities vary\ntogether. A positive correlation means that when one quantity is higher,\nthe other also tends to be higher. A negative correlation means the\nopposite direction. The <strong>Pearson correlation coefficient<\/strong>\n<code>r<\/code> takes values between <code>-1<\/code> and <code>+1<\/code>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r = 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans there is no <em>linear<\/em> relationship, but does not necessarily\nimply independence or the absence of non-linear relationships.<\/p>\n<p>Three possible explanations for an observed association must be\ndistinguished:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A causes B:<\/strong> All else being equal, a larger file\nsize can contribute to longer transfer times.<\/li>\n<li><strong>B influences A:<\/strong> The reverse direction may also be\nplausible in other situations.<\/li>\n<li><strong>C influences A and B:<\/strong> A third factor, such as high\nserver utilisation, may increase both the failure rate and response\ntime.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Chance, biased selection or a common trend over time are also\npossible explanations. <strong>Causality<\/strong> is a statement about\ncause and effect. Convincing causal claims require appropriate\ncomparison groups, temporal ordering and control of possible\nconfounders; randomised experiments are particularly informative in some\nsituations, but are not always possible or ethically acceptable.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"91-simpsons-paradoxon-alle-gruppen-besser-insgesamt-schlechter\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Simpson’s\nparadox: better in every group – worse overall?<\/h3>\n<p>A fictional test compares two algorithms. Algorithm B is more\nsuccessful <strong>within both difficulty groups<\/strong>, but is used\ndisproportionately for difficult cases:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Difficulty<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Algorithm A<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Success rate A<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Algorithm B<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Success rate B<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Easy<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">81 out of 90<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">90%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">19 out of 20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">95%<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Difficult<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1 out of 10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16 out of 80<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20%<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Overall<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>82 out of 100<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>82%<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>35 out of 100<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>35%<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>B leads in every group<\/strong>, yet A has the higher overall\nrate. The contradiction disappears when the <strong>different\ncomposition<\/strong> is considered: A handles predominantly easy cases,\nB predominantly difficult ones. This is called <em>Simpson’s\nparadox<\/em>. Without knowledge of group membership, a dashboard could\nsuggest the wrong technical decision.<\/p>\n<p>The right question is therefore not merely “Which rate is higher?”,\nbut also “Are the cases comparable in both groups?” In turn, the\ngrouping must not be arbitrarily chosen afterwards to produce a desired\nresult.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Time series, seasonality and changing systems<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>time series<\/strong> orders measurements by time. Daily\nwebsite accesses, hourly GPU utilisation and weekly failure rates are\ntypical examples. Such measurements have particular characteristics:\nweekly patterns, public holidays, growth, short-term outages and changes\nin measurement methods.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>trend<\/strong> is a longer-term direction,\n<strong>seasonality<\/strong> a recurring pattern and\n<strong>noise<\/strong> a short-term fluctuation. A <strong>moving\naverage<\/strong> smooths values over a rolling time window. It may make\na trend more apparent, but can conceal brief security incidents. An\nalert should not be based exclusively on a smoothed monthly average if\noutages lasting seconds are critical.<\/p>\n<p>A distinction must also be made between <strong>stock<\/strong> and\n<strong>flow<\/strong>: “Storage contains 500 GiB” is a stock at a point\nin time; “20 GiB were uploaded today” is a flow over a time interval.\nThe two cannot simply be summed using the same logic [3].<\/p>\n<p>For cloud services, reports must account for the correct <strong>time\nzone<\/strong>, daylight saving changes, late-arriving events and\nrepeated messages. Otherwise, apparent user growth may arise merely\nbecause a new log source was added. Comparable periods and consistent\ncounting definitions are prerequisites for a sound analysis of\nchange.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Good data visualisation: which chart matches the\nquestion?<\/h2>\n<p>A chart should clearly show a specific relationship. The UK\n<strong>Office for National Statistics<\/strong> (ONS) recommends making\nthe choice according to the data type and purpose of the statement and\nusing simple, familiar representations where possible [3].<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000096_en.png\"\nalt=\"Comparison of identical data with a full and a truncated y-axis\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of identical data with a full\nand a truncated y-axis<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>Suitable representation<\/th>\n<th>Typical pitfall<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Which category is larger?<\/td>\n<td>Bar chart<\/td>\n<td>Truncated zero baseline<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How does something change over time?<\/td>\n<td>Line chart<\/td>\n<td>Treating unequal time intervals as equal<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How are numbers distributed?<\/td>\n<td>Histogram or box plot<\/td>\n<td>Inappropriate class width<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How are two numbers related?<\/td>\n<td>Scatter plot<\/td>\n<td>Interpreting correlation as causation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What share of the whole does a group account for?<\/td>\n<td>Stacked bar or simple table<\/td>\n<td>Cluttered pie charts with many categories<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where do events occur?<\/td>\n<td>Map, if location is genuinely relevant<\/td>\n<td>Comparing area rather than normalised rates<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Bar charts<\/strong> use length to represent quantities and\nshould start at a zero baseline. If two bars for 98 and 102 units are\ndrawn starting at 95 rather than zero, the difference looks enormous:\nthe visible lengths would be 3 and 7 instead of 98 and 102. The numbers\ndiffer by only around <strong>4.08%<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>4<\/mn><mn>98<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac4{98}\\times100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nnot by more than a factor of two. For <strong>line charts<\/strong>,\njustified selections of the y-axis may be useful, provided labels and\ncontext make the selected range clear. This is different from a\ndeceptively truncated bar chart [4].<\/p>\n<p><strong>Histograms<\/strong> differ from ordinary bar charts: their\nclasses are intervals of numerical values. The choice of class width can\ncreate or hide two apparent peaks. <strong>Box plots<\/strong> show\ntypical location and dispersion, but may obscure the precise shape of a\nmultimodal distribution. Anyone looking for critical individual events\nneeds additional information.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Scatter plots<\/strong> show pairs of values and help identify\nnon-linear relationships or group differences. Overlapping points can\nconceal frequency; transparent points or aggregated densities may help.\nColoured points must be distinguishable even without colour\nperception.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Good charts are also accessible and traceable<\/h2>\n<p>A visualisation needs a factual title, clear axes,\n<strong>units<\/strong>, time period, data source and, where necessary,\nnotes about estimates or missing values. A title such as “Uploads by\nweek, January to March 2026” is more informative than “Performance”.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Accessibility<\/strong> is part of this: sufficient contrast,\ndistinction by more than colour alone, explanatory labels and a table or\ntextual summary of the key values. A screen reader can extract little\nfrom a graphic alone without accessible information; the insight should\ntherefore also be available in text form. This connects the elective to\nM9-L1 [5].<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>logarithmic axis<\/strong> can display several orders of\nmagnitude, but is harder for many readers to understand and should be\nexplicitly labelled. Two y-axes with different scales may suggest a\nstrong relationship that does not exist mathematically. A series of 2D\ncharts is often easier to read than a decorative 3D representation.<\/p>\n<p>On maps, the size of a region must not be confused with the frequency\nof an event. A large region may have more cases in absolute terms and\nstill have a lower <strong>rate per inhabitant<\/strong> than a small\nregion. A report should therefore often show both absolute numbers and\nnormalised rates.<\/p>\n<p>Before every publication, it is worth asking: <em>Which\ninterpretation is the chart likely to suggest at first glance – and is\nthat interpretation justified by the data?<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exploratory analysis, hypothesis tests and\nsignificance<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Exploratory data analysis<\/strong> (EDA) looks for structures\nand surprising observations. This is legitimate and useful, but an\nanomaly discovered afterwards should not be treated as if it had been\npredicted before examining the data.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>hypothesis test<\/strong> examines a clearly stated\nassumption using a statistical model. The <strong>p-value<\/strong>\ndescribes, under the assumed null hypothesis and model conditions, how\nunusual the data or more extreme results would be. It is\n<strong>not<\/strong> the probability that the null hypothesis is true,\nnor the probability that the finding is “just chance” [6].<\/p>\n<p>The widely used threshold\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p &lt; 0{,}05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a seal of truth. Trying a hundred charts or group comparisons and\nreporting only the most striking result encourages <strong>multiple\ntesting problems<\/strong> and <em>p-hacking<\/em>. A sound presentation\nspecifies the hypotheses investigated, selection rules, effect sizes,\nuncertainty and possible limitations. <strong>Statistical\nsignificance<\/strong> is not the same as <strong>practical\nrelevance<\/strong>: a tiny improvement may stand out statistically in\nvery large datasets and still yield less benefit than the operational\nand development costs.<\/p>\n<p>For decisions, the following is often more important: how large is\nthe estimated difference? What uncertainty remains? How costly is a\nwrong decision? Can the experiment be repeated? A good analysis may end\nwith “The data is not yet sufficient for this.”<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From A/B testing to a reliable decision<\/h2>\n<p>In an <strong>A/B test<\/strong>, groups that are as comparable as\npossible are randomly assigned to different variants. For example, users\nrandomly see an old or a new import interface. Measurements cover not\nonly how many click “Start”, but whether the task is actually completed\nsuccessfully and whether errors or abandonments occur.<\/p>\n<p>The outcome measure, target group, duration, planned comparison\nmethod and <strong>stopping conditions<\/strong> are specified in\nadvance. Random assignment should be defined persistently per person or\nsession so that the same person does not continually switch variants. If\ndevices, countries or usage times are unevenly distributed, the results\nmay be biased.<\/p>\n<p>An often overlooked point: <strong>the measurement must match the\ndecision.<\/strong> If variant B generates more clicks but fewer\nsuccessful uploads, the higher click rate does not constitute success.\nSimilarly, an improvement in average waiting time must not come at the\nexpense of severe outages or poorer accessibility.<\/p>\n<p>When looking at test results repeatedly each day, random fluctuations\nmay be tempting. A test should not be stopped simply because a desired\ninterim result briefly appears significant. An observational study can\nalso be valuable, provided it is not falsely presented as a randomised\nexperiment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data analysis and artificial intelligence: the same\nproblems, greater consequences<\/h2>\n<p>An AI model processes the same types of data quality problems on a\nlarger scale. A <strong>training dataset<\/strong> provides learning\nexamples; a <strong>validation dataset<\/strong> supports model selection\nand tuning; a <strong>test dataset<\/strong> is intended to enable final\nevaluation on previously unused cases. The split must not allow\ninformation from the test dataset to leak into training.<\/p>\n<p>This problem is called <strong>data leakage<\/strong>, or leakage\nbetween training and evaluation phases: if, for instance, the\nstandardisation of a variable is calculated using the mean of all data,\nincluding the test cases, test information influences model preparation.\nThis can artificially increase measured quality. The official\nscikit-learn documentation therefore recommends splitting the data\ncorrectly first and <em>learning<\/em> preprocessing only from the\ntraining data [7].<\/p>\n<p>Even seemingly unambiguous model metrics depend on context. M5-L3\nalready showed how high overall accuracy can be misleading for rare\nevents. When comparing two language models, it should additionally be\nclear whether the same tasks, identical assessment rules, model\nversions, output limits and random conditions were used. A change in\ndata composition may improve the mean although the model performs worse\nin a subject-critical group.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Data drift<\/strong> describes changes in the distribution of\ninput data, for example when small photos are suddenly replaced\npredominantly by high-resolution videos. <strong>Concept drift<\/strong>\nrefers to a change in the relationship between inputs and the outcome to\nbe predicted. Both can weaken a previously good model. Monitoring\nsystems may flag notable changes, but must distinguish genuine problems,\nseasonal patterns and changes in logging.<\/p>\n<p>A language model can formulate metrics or describe charts. However,\nit is <strong>not a substitute for traceable calculations on\ndata<\/strong>. If numbers are mentioned in an AI response, they should\ncome from verified data queries or calculation tools. An automatically\ngenerated management report therefore needs clear sources, dates,\nrounding rules and, where appropriate, human approval.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical architecture 1: understanding the local media\narchive<\/h2>\n<p>A private archive stores files, categories and import events. Its\noperator wants to know why importing large videos takes longer than\nimporting small images. Cloud analytics is not necessarily needed.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Local files / import logs\n             |\n             v\nSQLite or another local database\n             |\n             v\nChecks: units, duplicate attempts, timestamps\n             |\n             v\nGrouping by file type and size class\n             |\n             v\nMedian + quantiles + rare failure cases\n             |\n             v\nTable / simple report / chart<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The median import duration by file type shows typical runtimes,\nwhereas high quantiles and extreme cases can point to bottlenecks. A\nsingle average might obscure the large video import. If the same file\nhas been imported several times, the question determines whether each\nattempt or only the first successful import should be counted.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Useful<\/strong> when many files are processed and bottlenecks\nare no longer apparent from individual observations.\n<strong>Unnecessarily elaborate<\/strong> when there are only ten\nmanually managed files. A local database with a regularly generated\nreport may suffice; clusters, distributed data processing and GPU models\nwould create more maintenance than insight. Private filenames and\nmetadata need not be transferred to a third-party service merely for\nstatistics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical architecture 2: an online service with honest\nmetrics<\/h2>\n<p>A paid online analytics service wants to know how many jobs arrive,\nhow many are paid for and which actually end successfully. A typical\nprocess:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Website / API / billing\n          |\n          v\nEvents with a unique job ID\n          |\n          v\nJob database / controlled event log\n          |\n          v\nCleaned periodic analysis\n          |\n          +--&gt; technical metrics: failures, latency, load\n          |\n          +--&gt; business metrics: paid, completed\n          |\n          v\nDashboard with period, denominator, source, filter<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A payment event must not be counted three times because the payment\nservice has sent the same notification repeatedly. Equally, a completed\njob does not automatically mean a satisfied customer. Technical success\nrates, revenue metrics and user satisfaction measure different\nthings.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Useful<\/strong> metrics are small and traceable: the number\nof uniquely paid jobs, the completion rate per job started, the 95th\npercentile of runtime and clearly defined failure rates. A\n<strong>percentile<\/strong> indicates what proportion of values does not\nexceed a threshold – under an appropriate definition, approximately 95%\nare at or below the 95th percentile. The precise calculation conventions\nand measurement windows belong in the documentation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational risks:<\/strong> A public dashboard may reveal\ntrade secrets; small customer groups may potentially be identifiable.\nAccess controls, deletion rules, data minimisation and the separation of\nraw data from aggregated reports are important. An additional analytics\nplatform is only worthwhile when the volume of data, user needs and\nrequirements for timely results justify it.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical architecture 3: an AI service monitors its\nquality<\/h2>\n<p>A document-based AI assistant answers questions using company\ndocuments. Operators want to know whether new versions become\n<strong>more accurate<\/strong> and how often they produce unsupported\nclaims. A simple “Users gave it 4.6 stars” may reveal little about this:\nsatisfied users may only be rating the friendliness of the wording, not\nfactual correctness.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Versioned set of verified questions and evidence\n                    |\n                    v\nTest run with model version A and model version B\n                    |\n                    v\nAssessment: faithfulness to evidence, correctness,\n            refusal, runtime, costs and problematic outputs\n                    |\n                    v\nComparison by question group and difficulty level\n                    |\n                    v\nInterpretation + uncertainties + approval decision<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The <strong>evaluation data<\/strong> should not also have been known\nas training answers and used to tune the model. In addition, high-risk\nsubject-specific questions must not disappear in the average of a large\nnumber of easy questions. For example, average quality may rise while\nrare but important safety questions are answered incorrectly more\noften.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Useful<\/strong> measures include versioned test sets, defined\nassessment criteria, documented model parameters and human review of\ncritical outputs. <strong>It would not be sensible<\/strong> to use a\nsingle overall score as the basis for sweeping safety claims. Sensitive\noriginal documents or user data should not be copied into public\nanalytics services without examination. Computing and token costs\nincrease with the frequency and scope of tests; a targeted selection of\ninformative test cases is therefore often more economical than endless\nmeasurement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Costs, maintenance, data protection and accountability<\/h2>\n<p>Analytics has <strong>ongoing costs<\/strong>: data collection,\nstorage, cleaning, computing time, visualisation, checking, access\nmanagement and later adjustments. Technical quality also depends on who\ndefines a metric over the long term and who reviews changes to it.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>data catalogue<\/strong> or a concise <strong>metrics\nhandbook<\/strong> can record the name, definition,\nnumerator/denominator, units, filters, time window, data source, update\nfrequency, responsible persons and outstanding limitations. Such an\nentry is often more important than an expensive dashboard whose numbers\nno one can reliably explain.<\/p>\n<p>For personal information, the principles of the <strong>General Data\nProtection Regulation (GDPR)<\/strong> apply, including purpose\nlimitation and data minimisation. Not every technical identifier is\nharmless: IP addresses, device identifiers or pseudonymous user IDs may\nrelate to individuals. <strong>Anonymised<\/strong> does not simply mean\n“name removed”. Group statistics with very small case numbers can\ndisclose individual information. Data retention and access should\ntherefore be aligned with the legitimate purpose. Specific legal issues\nrequire separate assessment; this lesson is not a substitute for legal\nadvice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Fairness<\/strong> and <strong>responsibility<\/strong> also\nmatter. An overall analysis can hide systematic disadvantages for\nparticular user groups. At the same time, highly granular group analysis\nmay itself create data protection problems. Good practice seeks a\njustified balance: detecting relevant problems without creating\nunnecessary personal profiles.<\/p>\n<p>When data changes, comparability and <strong>versioning<\/strong> are\ncrucial. If the definition of an “active user” has changed, a time\nseries may need to be recalculated or clearly interrupted. An alarming\njump in the chart may merely reflect the introduction of new tracking. A\ndata analysis process should therefore accommodate audit logs, known\nlimitations and follow-up questions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When is a table sufficient – and when is more needed?<\/h2>\n<p>Not every data question requires machine learning, a central data\nlake or a complex dashboard. For a few hundred local records, an SQL\nquery, a table and a carefully explained chart are often sufficient. An\n<strong>SQL query<\/strong> (<em>Structured Query Language<\/em>) can, for\nexample, group and count jobs by state; however, the substantive\nquestion is independent of any programming language.<\/p>\n<p>Larger analytics environments become interesting when data from many\nsystems needs to be combined, changes must be tracked continuously or\nnumerous reports have to be generated consistently. Data pipelines,\nscheduled processing, versioning and possibly distributed computing\nmethods from M4-L1 then come into play. <strong>Scaling<\/strong> does\nnot create correct definitions: a billion incorrectly classified events\nproduce a very large report, but not automatically a correct one.<\/p>\n<p>A sensible decision sequence is:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Is there a clearly formulated question and a meaningful reference\nquantity?<\/li>\n<li>Are the data sufficiently complete and correctly defined?<\/li>\n<li>Which simple metric or table would answer the question?<\/li>\n<li>What can <em>not<\/em> be concluded from it?<\/li>\n<li>Does a graphic help, or is it merely decorative?<\/li>\n<li>Do the results need regular monitoring, checking and\nprotection?<\/li>\n<li>Is the additional operational effort justified by the actual gain in\nknowledge?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Classification, key principles and connections to\nmathematics<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Data consists of measurements with an origin and a\nmeaning.<\/strong> The appropriate average depends on the question. A\npercentage is incomplete without a denominator. Outliers require\ninvestigation rather than reflexive deletion. Small samples produce\ngreater uncertainty; systematic errors do not disappear even with very\nlarge samples. Correlation does not establish causation, and a\nstatistically significant figure is not automatically important in\npractice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Good visualisation<\/strong> makes comparisons readable,\npreserves proportions and states the source, period and uncertainty.\n<strong>Good AI evaluation<\/strong> accounts for representative test\ncases, data leakage, rare errors, different user groups and actual\noperational costs. The same critical perspective helps when reading news\nstatistics, making product decisions and examining scientific\npublications.<\/p>\n<p>For further mathematical study, School (German-language) M1-L12 on\ncombinatorics, School M1-L22 on conditional probability and University\nM1-L5 on linear algebra are also suitable. Computer Science M8-L1\nintroduces sets and functions. This lesson focuses on data pipelines,\nsystem boundaries and the interpretation of results.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Outlook:<\/strong> M9-L3 applies computer science to embedded\ndevices, sensors and microcontrollers. This creates a direct connection\nbetween measurement errors, sensor data and the data checks learnt in\nthis chapter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S22\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Position within the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This is an <strong>optional Bachelor elective lesson<\/strong>, not an\nadditional main-course lesson. The conceptual bridge leads, among other\nplaces, to <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L5 in the Master\ncore<\/a>. Further routes: <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course-wide reading paths<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course index<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S23\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Checked on 04.11.2026.<\/strong> The following original or\nofficial specialist sources support the concepts, basic rules and limits\nof interpretation. All numerical examples in this lesson are explicitly\n<strong>original, fictional teaching data<\/strong>, not measurements\nfrom these sources.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>NIST / SEMATECH:<\/strong> <em>Engineering Statistics\nHandbook<\/em>, “Measures of Location” (mean, median, mode). <a\nhref=\"https://itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda351.htm\">https://itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda351.htm<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>U.S. Census Bureau:<\/strong> <em>Survey of Construction –\nHow the Data are Collected<\/em>, section “Reliability of Data” (sampling\nand non-sampling errors). <a\nhref=\"https://www.census.gov/construction/soc/methodology.html\">https://www.census.gov/construction/soc/methodology.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>UK Office for National Statistics (ONS):<\/strong>\n<em>Choosing a chart type<\/em> (relationship to the question, time\nseries, stock/flow). <a\nhref=\"https://service-manual.ons.gov.uk/data-visualisation/chart-types/choosing-a-chart-type\">https://service-manual.ons.gov.uk/data-visualisation/chart-types/choosing-a-chart-type<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>ONS:<\/strong> <em>Axes and gridlines<\/em> (zero baseline for\nbar charts, consistent scales, limitations of dual axes). <a\nhref=\"https://service-manual.ons.gov.uk/data-visualisation/guidance/axes-and-gridlines\">https://service-manual.ons.gov.uk/data-visualisation/guidance/axes-and-gridlines<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>ONS:<\/strong> <em>Using colours in charts<\/em>\n(accessibility and comprehensible colour coding). <a\nhref=\"https://service-manual.ons.gov.uk/data-visualisation/colours/using-colours-in-charts\">https://service-manual.ons.gov.uk/data-visualisation/colours/using-colours-in-charts<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>American Statistical Association (ASA):<\/strong>\nWasserstein/Lazar (2016), <em>The ASA Statement on p-Values: Context,\nProcess, and Purpose<\/em>, DOI: 10.1080/00031305.2016.1154108. <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.2016.1154108\">https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.2016.1154108<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>scikit-learn:<\/strong> <em>Common pitfalls and recommended\npractices<\/em> (data leakage in preprocessing and model evaluation). <a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html\">https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Limits of the lesson:<\/strong> The confidence interval\ncalculation assumes a simple model; Simpson’s paradox is a didactic\nconstruction; no number describes real providers, users or system\nperformance. Actual research requires study design, quality control and\nappropriate specialist statistical methods.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M9-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M9-L3 – Embedded systems, sensors and microcontrollers<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor’s: Elective and practical areas<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor (elective / application)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Optional Bachelor elective topic\n(M9-L3)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading path:<\/strong> M1-L2 (computer architecture), M1-L6\n(networks), M8-L3 (operating systems and concurrency) and M6-L7\n(robotics) are useful. However, the lesson can be read without these\ntexts. It requires neither the purchase of electronics, programming\nexercises nor installation. The connection to mathematics concerns\nfunctions, quantities and units, vectors, simple rates of change and,\nlater, differential equations. Specific module identifiers in the\nseparate mathematics course have not been verified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A computer that is intended to perform a specific task<\/h2>\n<p>A washing machine must control a motor and take water levels into\naccount. A smoke detector must respond to a particular event. A digital\ncamera must read its image sensor long before the image might be edited\non another computer. Computer science is embedded in such devices, even\nif they have no desk, screen or web browser.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>embedded system<\/strong> is a computing system integrated\ninto a larger technical device or process, where it performs specific\ntasks. The task limits which hardware and software are appropriate. Some\nembedded systems run for years on a few milliwatts; others process\ncamera images on powerful chips. They therefore need not be small, but\nusually have more clearly defined tasks and constraints than a\ngeneral-purpose PC.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>microcontroller<\/strong> is a common, but not the only,\nfoundation for such systems. It combines a processor, working memory and\nmany input/output functions on a single chip. A <strong>sensor<\/strong>\nmeasures a physical quantity such as temperature, light or movement. An\n<strong>actuator<\/strong> affects the environment, for example a motor,\nheater or valve. Between them are software, electrical interfaces, a\npower supply and often protective mechanisms.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Physical environment\n       |                      ^\n       v                      |\n  Sensor / switch        Actuator / motor\n       |                      ^\n       v                      |\nMeasurement / input -&gt; Microcontroller -&gt; Control signal\n                           |\n                     Program state\n                           |\n                  optional: network / cloud<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The most important perspective is <strong>feedback<\/strong>: not\nmerely processing data, but dealing with consequences in the real world.\nIf a computer outputs incorrect numbers, this is inconvenient. If it\nopens a valve at the wrong time, the damage may be physical. Fault\nstates, power supply and safe shutdown therefore belong to the\narchitecture, not merely to the documentation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Microcontrollers, microprocessors and single-board\ncomputers<\/h2>\n<p>An ordinary desktop processor usually requires external memory\nmodules, a complex chipset and an operating system. A microcontroller is\nmore highly integrated. Its program can start directly when power is\nswitched on, without a graphical interface. A <strong>single-board\ncomputer<\/strong> (SBC) such as a Raspberry Pi running Linux, by\ncontrast, resembles a small, complete PC. Both can read sensors, but\ntheir strengths differ.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Architecture<\/th>\n<th>Typical strength<\/th>\n<th>Common limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Microcontroller (MCU)<\/td>\n<td>Low energy use, direct peripherals, readily controlled\nresponses<\/td>\n<td>Little RAM and computing power, restricted software environment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Microprocessor/SoC with Linux<\/td>\n<td>Large applications, network services, image processing<\/td>\n<td>More complex start-up, higher energy needs, usually no hard\nreal-time guarantee<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Specialised circuit<\/td>\n<td>Very precise or fast individual task<\/td>\n<td>Less flexible, more costly to modify<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>MCU<\/strong> stands for <em>Microcontroller Unit<\/em>.\n<strong>SoC<\/strong> means <em>System on a Chip<\/em>, a chip integrating\nseveral computing and functional blocks. The terms overlap: some modern\nSoCs contain both powerful processor cores and small real-time cores.\nThere is no rigid boundary.<\/p>\n<p>The Raspberry Pi <strong>RP2040<\/strong> serves as a concrete,\nnon-prescriptive product example. According to the official datasheet,\nit has two Arm Cortex-M0+ cores, 264 KiB of on-chip SRAM and hardware\nblocks for, among others, I²C, SPI, UART and PWM. The program is\ntypically stored in external flash memory. The precise design of a board\ndepends on its circuitry. These figures describe <strong>this one\nproduct<\/strong>, not all microcontrollers. <a\nhref=\"https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/rp2040/specifications/\">Source:\nRaspberry Pi, RP2040 specification, checked 04.11.2026<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is a microcontroller unnecessary?<\/strong> For an\nexisting website that collects data and displays charts, a server often\nsuffices. An additional sensor chip solves no problem there. Conversely,\na complete Linux computer would often be unnecessarily complex for a\nsingle battery-powered door contact sensor.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is inside the chip?<\/h2>\n<p>A microcontroller normally has a processor core, registers, working\nmemory and various <strong>peripheral blocks<\/strong>. Peripherals are\nspecialised functions that need not be executed as general-purpose\nprogram code. For example, a timer counts clock pulses while the\nprocessor does other things. A communication block transmits bits\naccording to a specified protocol. An analogue-to-digital converter\nprocesses voltages.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/000063_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A sensor supplies a measurement signal to a microcontroller, which sends a control signal to an actuator. The power supply is shown separately using dashed lines.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A sensor supplies a measurement signal to\na microcontroller, which sends a control signal to an actuator. The\npower supply is shown separately using dashed lines.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The sensor, microcontroller and actuator form a measurement and\ncontrol path.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                 MICROCONTROLLER\n +--------------------------------------------------+\n | CPU core(s)       RAM       Flash / flash access |\n |      |             |              |              |\n |      +---------- internal bus ----+              |\n |                         |                        |\n | GPIO    Timer/PWM    ADC    I²C/SPI/UART           |\n |  |         |          |        |                 |\n +--|---------|----------|--------|-----------------+\n    |         |          |        |\n Switch     Motor      Analogue  Digital\n /LED       driver     sensor    sensor<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>RAM<\/strong> (<em>Random Access Memory<\/em>) is volatile\nworking memory. <strong>Flash<\/strong> is non-volatile memory for\nprograms and persistent data. Not every chip has internal program\nmemory; on the RP2040 mentioned above, the code is usually stored in\nexternal flash. Some microcontrollers have additional non-volatile\nmemory cells, others do not. Available RAM is often so small that\nbuffering entire files without checking their size is not an option.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Registers<\/strong> are small, rapidly accessible storage\nlocations. Particular registers control hardware: a bit can, for\nexample, determine whether a pin is an input or an output. In practice,\napplications often use driver libraries for this purpose. The driver\ntranslates a general instruction such as “read sensor value” into the\nrequired register accesses and transmissions.<\/p>\n<p>Software thus need not manage every electrical detail itself.\nNevertheless, knowledge of hardware limits remains important: two pins\nmay require the same internal function; a communication block may\ncurrently be busy; a memory buffer may be full. Such bottlenecks cannot\nbe resolved simply by programming faster.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Electrical signals: digital and analogue<\/h2>\n<p>Computers calculate using discrete states. The physical world, by\ncontrast, often changes continuously. A lamp can grow brighter,\ntemperature rises gradually and a motor rotates at varying speeds.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>digital input signal<\/strong> is interpreted as a logical\nstate, for example “0” or “1”. This does not necessarily mean exactly\nzero or another specific voltage. The chip distinguishes valid low and\nhigh levels using electrical thresholds. Ambiguous levels, excessive\nvoltages or missing ground connections may cause malfunctions or damage.\nThe limits are stated in the particular datasheet, not in a universally\napplicable 3.3-volt rule.<\/p>\n<p><strong>GPIO<\/strong> stands for <em>General-Purpose\nInput/Output<\/em>, meaning universally usable input/output pins. A GPIO\ncan, for example, read the state of a button or set a digital signal.\n<strong>Pull-up or pull-down resistors<\/strong> establish a defined\ndefault state so that an unconnected input does not fluctuate randomly.\nA mechanical button may briefly “bounce” when pressed: its contact\nchanges several times in a very short period. Software or additional\ncircuitry can therefore <strong>debounce<\/strong> the signal.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>analogue signal<\/strong> may take many voltage values\nwithin a range. An <strong>analogue-to-digital converter (ADC,\nAnalog-to-Digital Converter)<\/strong> converts it into a digital\nmeasurement code. It is limited by resolution, input range, sampling\nrate, noise and other characteristics. A converter with higher\nresolution does not automatically measure more accurately. Conversely, a\ndigital temperature sensor can calculate its own measured value and\ntransmit it via I²C; the main controller then does not need to read an\nanalogue voltage.<\/p>\n<p>The principle can be understood without hardware:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Temperature -&gt; sensing element -&gt; electrical quantity\n                                      |\n                      +---------------+---------------+\n                      |                               |\n                      v                               v\n              Analogue voltage                  Digital sensor\n                      |                               |\n               ADC in controller                Measurement chip with its\n                      |                         own conversion and protocol\n                      +---------------+---------------+\n                                      |\n                                Measured value<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A measured value becomes a physical statement only when its\n<strong>unit, scaling and calibration<\/strong> are known. “Value 1800”\nmight be an ADC number, but not a temperature. Even correct software\ncannot magically improve a wrongly mounted, heated or poorly calibrated\nsensor.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Numerical example: what does twelve-bit measurement\nresolution mean?<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine an idealised ADC with <strong>12 bits<\/strong> and a\nreference range of <strong>0 to 3.3 volts<\/strong>. Twelve bits allow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>12<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>096<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{12}=4\\,096<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndifferent codes, usually from 0 to 4095. The calculated distance between\nadjacent quantisation levels is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>096<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0008056640625<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{3{,}3\\,\\mathrm{V}}{4\\,096}=0{,}0008056640625\\,\\mathrm{V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor around <strong>0.806 millivolts<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>If the converter measures code 2048, for example, a simple idealised\nmapping gives approximately:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>048<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>096<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>65<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{2\\,048\\times3{,}3\\,\\mathrm{V}}{4\\,096}=1{,}65\\,\\mathrm{V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The number 2048 therefore means <em>approximately half the reference\nvoltage<\/em>. It is not proof that exactly 1.650000 volts are present at\nthe input. In real ADCs, the <strong>effective number of bits\n(ENOB)<\/strong> may be lower than the nominal 12-bit resolution because\nof noise and distortion. ENOB is a metric of dynamic converter\nperformance under particular measurement conditions, not simply the\nnumber of output bits; for precision measurements, further quantities\nsuch as noise and calibration must be considered separately. The actual\nconversion may be implemented differently according to the datasheet,\ncalibration procedure, endpoint definition and circuitry; sensor and\nconverter errors also apply. The example initially describes only a\n<strong>theoretical resolution<\/strong>, not a guaranteed\n<strong>measurement accuracy<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Three concepts here must not be confused:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Resolution:<\/strong> How finely can the measurement range be\nsubdivided digitally?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Accuracy:<\/strong> How close is the measurement to the true\nvalue?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Precision/repeatability:<\/strong> How much do repeated\nmeasurements vary under comparable conditions?<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Noise, supply voltage, temperature drift, cable length, interference\nfrom a motor and quantisation may affect the measurement. Measuring\nseveral times and averaging can reduce random fluctuations, but does not\nautomatically eliminate a systematic calibration error.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Sampling rate<\/strong> is the number of measurements per unit\nof time. A temperature sensor may need only one value per minute. Much\nmore frequent measurements are necessary to analyse rapid oscillations.\nAccording to the Nyquist–Shannon sampling theorem, a suitably\nband-limited signal theoretically requires more than two samples per\nperiod of the highest frequency it contains to make complete\nreconstruction possible in principle. Practical systems also require\nsuitable filters, sufficient quality and often a safety margin.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Outputs, PWM and actuators<\/h2>\n<p>A microcontroller pin can supply digital signals, but <strong>cannot\npower arbitrary loads<\/strong>. A motor, solenoid valve or powerful\nheater typically needs a suitable driver, a separate power stage and\nprotective circuits. A motor connected directly to an unsuitable pin can\ndamage the microcontroller. For a reading-only concept, it is enough to\nunderstand that necessary power electronics lie between “the software\nrequests movement” and “the motor moves”.<\/p>\n<p><strong>PWM<\/strong> stands for <em>Pulse Width Modulation<\/em>. An\noutput switches very rapidly between two states. The proportion of\non-time within one period is called the <strong>duty cycle<\/strong>.\nWith an ideal 25 per cent duty cycle, the output is on for one quarter\nof every period. With suitable circuitry and a suitable load, this can\nbe used, for example, to influence the average energy supplied and thus\nthe brightness of a light-emitting diode.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>PWM period:    |---------|---------|\nSignal on:     |██       |██       |\nSignal off:    |  _______|  _______|\n                20% on    20% on<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The illustrated signal is for demonstration only. The duty cycle must\ncorrespond to the actual pulse width. For motors, the effect also\ndepends on inductance, driver, load and feedback control. PWM frequency\nis a separate quantity: a 50 per cent duty cycle does not reveal whether\nthere are a hundred or ten thousand periods per second.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Closed-loop control<\/strong> differs from simple open-loop\ncontrol. In open-loop control, an output is specified. Closed-loop\ncontrol additionally measures the effect and repeatedly adjusts the\noutput. A thermostat compares temperature with a setpoint. A motor\ncontroller compares measured and desired rotational speed. If the sensor\nbecomes implausible, the system must determine which reaction is safe.\nThis differs according to the device and requires its own safety\nanalysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How devices communicate: GPIO, UART, I²C, SPI and CAN<\/h2>\n<p>Many components are not useful as isolated units. A microcontroller\nmust communicate with sensors, displays, memory chips and other control\nunits. Electrical interfaces and <strong>communication\nprotocols<\/strong> provide this: agreements on how bits are transmitted\nand understood.<\/p>\n<p><strong>UART<\/strong> means <em>Universal Asynchronous\nReceiver-Transmitter<\/em>. It is a widely used form of serial\ntransmission without a shared transmitted clock line. Transmitter and\nreceiver must use compatible settings, such as data rate and frame\nformat. The UART electronics alone do not determine the meaning of a\nreceived byte.<\/p>\n<p><strong>I²C<\/strong> stands for <em>Inter-Integrated Circuit<\/em>. It\nis a shared bus with normally one data line and one clock line. A\ncontroller communicates with addressed devices. Several sensors can\nshare the lines if their addresses, bus loading and electrical design\nare appropriate. The terms “Controller” and “Target” correspond to the\ncurrent documentation; older texts often use “Master” and “Slave”.<\/p>\n<p><strong>SPI<\/strong> means <em>Serial Peripheral Interface<\/em>.\nCommon variants use a clock, data lines and a select line for each\naddressed device. Many SPI connections are suitable for fast\nperipherals; however, they are not automatically suitable for long\ncables.<\/p>\n<p><strong>CAN<\/strong> stands for <em>Controller Area Network<\/em>. It\nis important in vehicle and industrial environments because several\nparticipants can communicate via a shared bus and prioritise messages.\nSeparate specifications apply to physical connections, data integrity\nand error signalling. A CAN message is not inherently encrypted or\nauthenticated against malicious participants.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interface<\/th>\n<th>Broad strength<\/th>\n<th>Typical limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>GPIO<\/td>\n<td>Direct, simple signal<\/td>\n<td>No complete data protocol<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UART<\/td>\n<td>Straightforward point-to-point data<\/td>\n<td>Framing and payload format must be agreed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I²C<\/td>\n<td>Several peripheral devices on a shared bus<\/td>\n<td>Addressing, electrical load and cable length must be considered<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SPI<\/td>\n<td>Often fast peripheral communication<\/td>\n<td>Additional lines; precise characteristics depend on the device<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN<\/td>\n<td>Robust bus communication in the right environment<\/td>\n<td>Interface hardware and higher-level protocols required<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The choice depends on the <strong>sensor and environment<\/strong>,\nnot on a universal winning technology. Zephyr’s official driver overview\ndescribes separate APIs for GPIO, ADC, I²C, SPI, CAN and PWM, for\nexample. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/hardware/peripherals/index.html\">Source:\nZephyr Project, Peripherals, checked 04.11.2026<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Polling, interrupts and DMA<\/h2>\n<p>A program could check a hundred times per second: “Has the button\nbeen pressed?” This is called <strong>polling<\/strong>, or repeated\nquerying. It is simple, but may consume computing time if poorly\ndesigned and may miss brief events. An <strong>interrupt<\/strong>, by\ncontrast, is a request to interrupt the processor, for example when an\ninput changes state or a timer expires. The CPU then executes an\nappropriate short <strong>interrupt handler<\/strong> and resumes its\nnormal work.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Normal operation: evaluate readings ---&gt; update memory\n                              |\nButton event -----------------+----&gt; Interrupt\n                                     |\n                                     v\n                                 Record event\n                                     |\n                                     v\n                                 Resume normal work\n                                     |\n                                     v\n                                 Handle event safely<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>It is important to distinguish <strong>rapid response<\/strong> from\n<strong>extensive processing<\/strong>. As a rule, an interrupt handler\nshould remain short, save critical data and hand further work over to a\nnormal part of the program or an operating system task. Long\ncalculations or uncontrolled waiting times within an interrupt may block\nother events. The particular software environment determines which\nfunctions are permitted within an interrupt.<\/p>\n<p>Many Arm Cortex-M processors use the <strong>Nested Vectored\nInterrupt Controller (NVIC)<\/strong> for this purpose. It supports the\nassignment of interrupt sources and priorities; details vary between\nCortex-M variants. <a\nhref=\"https://arm-software.github.io/CMSIS_6/main/Core/group__NVIC__gr.html\">Source:\nArm CMSIS-Core, Interrupts and Exceptions, checked 04.11.2026<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>DMA<\/strong> stands for <em>Direct Memory Access<\/em>:\nsuitable hardware transfers data between peripherals and memory without\nthe processor having to copy each byte individually. This can save\ncomputing time. However, it requires correct coordination of buffers,\ndata consistency and transfer completion. DMA does not automatically\nmake a faulty application safe.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Time is not just speed: real time<\/h2>\n<p>A system is not <strong>real-time capable<\/strong> merely because it\nusually responds quickly. In real-time computing, <strong>time\nlimits<\/strong> within which a response is required are what matters. A\nmeasurement, for example, must be evaluated by a specified time at the\nlatest. A poor response time may be more serious than an algorithm that\nis slower on average.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>deadline<\/strong> is such a time limit.\n<strong>Latency<\/strong> refers to the delay between an event and the\nresponse. <strong>Jitter<\/strong> is the variation in this delay. A\ndistinction is often made between <strong>hard real time<\/strong>, where\nexceeding a deadline counts as system failure, and <strong>soft real\ntime<\/strong>, where individual late results may be tolerated in some\ncircumstances.<\/p>\n<p>This can be distinguished on three temporal levels:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Sensor detects obstacle\n        |\n        v\nLocal interrupt / measurement  as short and bounded as possible\n        |\n        v\nLocal safety function           fixed requirements, independent of cloud\n        |\n        v\nMotor control / driver         defined response chain\n        |\n        +----&gt; optional: logging -&gt; Wi-Fi -&gt; cloud analytics<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The cloud can collect states or recommend a route. For a machine with\ncorresponding safety-critical requirements, however, it must not be the\nonly entity responsible for stopping the machine when danger suddenly\narises. Network delays and interruptions to wireless connections cannot\nbe ruled out in principle. A language model likewise provides no\nuniversally guaranteed real-time response.<\/p>\n<p>In practice, a maximum response time comprises several components:\nsensor detection, data transmission, scheduling, processing, output\ndriver and mechanical effect. Even if the CPU responds within one\nmillisecond, a moving mechanism may take considerably longer to stop. A\nreal safety concept requires measurements, protective devices,\nassessment against standards and specialised professional expertise;\nthis lesson provides <strong>no<\/strong> approval for real machine\ncontrols.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Does a microcontroller need an operating system?<\/h2>\n<p>Not always. A simple program can run in a main loop after power-on:\nit reads the sensor, updates states, controls outputs and begins again.\nThis is often called <strong>bare metal<\/strong>, because the\napplication runs directly on the hardware. A small <strong>state\nmachine<\/strong> can already organise all relevant processes, for\nexample <code>RUHE -&gt; MESSEN -&gt; PRUEFEN -&gt; MELDEN<\/code> (idle,\nmeasure, check, report).<\/p>\n<p>As soon as several activities require different response times, a\n<strong>real-time operating system (RTOS)<\/strong> may be useful. It\nmanages tasks, priorities, timers and synchronisation mechanisms. Unlike\na full desktop operating system, a small RTOS often focuses on readily\ncontrollable embedded tasks.<\/p>\n<p>In its documented standard configuration on a single core,\n<strong>FreeRTOS<\/strong> uses pre-emptive priority scheduling and time\nslices for tasks with equal priority. This is configurable and differs\nin multicore variants. A high-priority task may starve others if it\nremains continuously ready instead of waiting for events. <a\nhref=\"https://github.freertos.org/Documentation/02-Kernel/02-Kernel-features/01-Tasks-and-co-routines/04-Task-scheduling\">Source:\nFreeRTOS, Task Scheduling, October 2026, checked 04.11.2026<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Zephyr<\/strong> is another real-time operating system\necosystem with hardware abstractions, device drivers, network functions\nand power-saving mechanisms. Its APIs cover GPIO, ADC, I²C, SPI and\nsensors, among other things. It illustrates how software can support\ndifferent hardware platforms through shared interfaces. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/doxygen/html/\">Source:\nZephyr Project, API documentation, checked 04.11.2026<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>An RTOS does not relieve an application of every responsibility.\n<strong>Race conditions<\/strong>, locked resources, blocking drivers and\ninappropriate priorities may still occur. A task scheduler is not a\nformal proof that every deadline will be met either. Here M9-L3 links\ndirectly to M8-L3 on operating systems and concurrency.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Power supply and sleep modes<\/h2>\n<p>A power supply continuously powers a device; a battery-powered\nsensor, by contrast, must cope with a limited energy reserve. This leads\nto a different optimisation question: not necessarily “How fast does the\nprocessor calculate?”, but “How briefly can it remain awake?”.<\/p>\n<p>A typical distinction is made between <strong>active\noperation<\/strong>, <strong>idle<\/strong>, <strong>sleep<\/strong> and\ndeeper power-saving modes. During sleep, parts of the chip are switched\noff. A timer or an appropriate external signal may wake it later. Memory\ncontents, wake-up sources and restart times must be considered. Some\ndevices have a low-power coprocessor or a separate real-time clock.<\/p>\n<p>The basic model is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Average current<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">active fraction<\/mtext><mo>×<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">active current<\/mtext><mo>+<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">sleep fraction<\/mtext><mo>×<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">sleep current<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{Average current}=\\text{active fraction}\\times\\text{active current}+\\text{sleep fraction}\\times\\text{sleep current}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Numerical example, explicitly fictional:<\/strong> A sensor is\nactive for one second every minute and requires 20 milliamperes (mA)\nwhile active. It sleeps for the remaining 59 seconds at 0.05 mA. This\ngives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>60<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>59<\/mn><mn>60<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>05<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\mathrm{mittel}}=\\frac1{60}\\times20\\,\\mathrm{mA}+\\frac{59}{60}\\times0{,}05\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3333<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0492<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3825<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\mathrm{mittel}}=0{,}3333\\,\\mathrm{mA}+0{,}0492\\,\\mathrm{mA}\\approx0{,}3825\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>An idealised battery with 2000 milliampere-hours (mAh) would\ngive:<\/p>\n<p><code>2000 mAh / 0,3825 mA = approximately 5229 hours<\/code>, or\n<strong>about 218 days<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Actual operating time may differ considerably: batteries do not\nalways deliver their nominal capacity in cold conditions, as they age or\nduring high current peaks. A radio module, illuminated display, sensor\nheater or voltage converter may consume more energy than the\nmicrocontroller itself. Sleep and wake-up phases must also meet safety\nrequirements. A device that must detect a dangerous event at any time\ncannot simply switch off the corresponding sensor.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:<\/strong> Milliamperes (mA) measure\ncurrent; milliampere-hours (mAh) describe a quantity of electric charge.\nA correct consideration of energy additionally requires the voltage and\nactual losses. The quotient of mAh and mA represents a duration of use\nonly under the simplified assumptions given.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>State machines, timers and robust software<\/h2>\n<p>Many embedded programs can be understood better as <strong>state\nmachines<\/strong> than as a long list of commands. A water detector\nmight have the states <code>NORMAL<\/code>, <code>VERDACHT<\/code>,\n<code>ALARM<\/code> and <code>STOERUNG<\/code> (normal, suspected event,\nalarm and fault). Each state defines what the system is permitted to do\nand how it responds to new events.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>NORMAL -- unusual reading ----&gt; VERDACHT\n   ^                               |\n   |                               | Reading confirmed\n   |                               v\n   +-- stable normal reading --- ALARM\n                                   |\n                                acknowledged?\n                                   |\n                                   v\n                                NORMAL\n\nAny state -- invalid sensor reading --&gt; STOERUNG<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The diagram is simplified for teaching; in a real alarm device,\nrecovery from an alarm depends on the safety requirement. A missing\nsensor reading must not necessarily be interpreted as “everything is\nnormal”. <strong>Plausibility checks<\/strong> can detect when a\ntemperature jumps by a physically implausible amount, data is absent or\ntwo sensors strongly contradict one another.<\/p>\n<p>Timers replace unreliable waiting loops. A <strong>watchdog<\/strong>\nis a monitoring mechanism that can detect a stalled program and trigger\na restart. A watchdog does not prevent every fault, however. If a\ndefective device performs the same incorrect action after every restart,\nrestarting automatically is not a safe solution.<\/p>\n<p>During a <strong>brownout<\/strong>, the supply voltage falls below a\ncritical threshold. Appropriate undervoltage monitoring should prevent\nthe processor from continuing to operate under impermissible conditions.\nStorage must also be considered: a power failure in the middle of\nwriting may corrupt data. Strategies range from checksums and duplicated\nrecords to transaction-like storage patterns. Such measures must suit\nthe particular flash memory and its limited write cycles.<\/p>\n<p>A good design asks for every failure: <strong>What does the user\nnotice? What remains stored? What safe state is possible\nafterwards?<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sensor fusion and calibration<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor provides an observation, not absolute truth. A light sensor\nreading depends on its viewing direction, contamination and lighting. An\naccelerometer measures, among other things, the effects of gravity and\nmovement. A single distance measurement may be affected by reflections\nor obstructed surfaces.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Sensor fusion<\/strong> combines different information to\nestimate a system’s state more reliably. In a moving device, for\nexample, a gyroscope, accelerometer and wheel encoder may work together.\nA <strong>gyroscope<\/strong> senses rotational movements; an\n<strong>encoder<\/strong> can convert wheel or motor movement into count\npulses. The mathematical combination ranges from simple weighted means\nto Kalman filters and non-linear techniques. These advanced methods are\nsuitable for additional Master-level study of robotics and control\nengineering.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Calibration<\/strong> relates sensor values to known\nquantities. A temperature sensor may have a systematic offset; a\nmeasurement bridge may react to changes in supply voltage. More sensors\nonly help if their errors are sufficiently independent and their\ninstallation conditions are appropriate. Two identically misinstalled\nsensors do not provide a reliable double check.<\/p>\n<p>Even a simple distinction avoids many misunderstandings: <strong>raw\ndata<\/strong> is the direct measurement codes; <strong>measured\nvalues<\/strong> are interpreted quantities with units; <strong>state\nestimates<\/strong> combine measurements and model assumptions;\n<strong>control decisions<\/strong> are conclusions with effects. These\nlevels should remain separate in the documentation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Communication beyond the local device<\/h2>\n<p>A microcontroller can work completely offline. If it is networked,\nhowever, it may be part of the <strong>Internet of Things\n(IoT)<\/strong>. This does not mean that every IoT device should be\ndirectly reachable from the public Internet. A local gateway is often a\nbetter solution: it translates device protocols, collects readings and\nprovides only selected information to a server.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Sensors -&gt; Microcontroller -&gt; Local gateway\n                                    |\n                          Authenticated connection\n                                    |\n                                    v\n                              Server / database\n                                    |\n                                    v\n                                 Dashboard<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Two paths must be distinguished: <strong>telemetry<\/strong> transmits\nmeasurements externally; <strong>control commands<\/strong> change the\ndevice. For a simple temperature history, occasionally sending data\nsuffices. For heating or door controls, stricter rules are needed\nconcerning who may issue commands, how old commands are recognised and\nwhat happens if the connection fails.<\/p>\n<p><strong>MQTT<\/strong>, for example, is suitable for device messages:\na device publishes messages on a topic, and interested participants\nsubscribe to that topic. Depending on the delivery level, the same\nmessage may arrive several times. The protocol replaces neither identity\nverification nor domain-specific plausibility checks. The principles of\nidempotence, sequence numbers and state reconciliation from the course\nlessons on distributed systems also apply here.<\/p>\n<p>The assumption “If the server reports nothing, everything must be\nfine” is particularly dangerous. If the network fails, the device must\nknow what it may continue doing locally. A smoke-detecting device must\nnot depend on a cloud response to trigger a necessary local alarm\nfunction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security, updates and data protection over the years<\/h2>\n<p>Embedded devices often remain in use for a long time. This is their\nstrength and also a security problem. A delivered device may still need\nbug fixes years later. <strong>Firmware<\/strong> here refers to software\nclose to the hardware that is not usually replaced in everyday use like\nan ordinary desktop application.<\/p>\n<p>A sound security concept considers not only the encrypted network\nconnection, but also <strong>device identities, secure configuration,\nprotected credentials, update mechanisms, logging and physical\naccess<\/strong>. An outdated default password or openly accessible\nmaintenance interface can undermine the other measures. Where the\nhardware supports them, secure boot and signed updates can help detect\nmanipulated software; this does not mean that every chip automatically\nprovides secure key storage.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Over-the-air updates (OTA)<\/strong> are software updates\ndelivered via a connection. They must handle interrupted downloads,\nunexpected restarts and faulty packages. A device can use two firmware\nareas for this purpose, allowing it to revert to an earlier version\nfollowing a faulty update. This technique requires memory and careful\nimplementation. Critical applications additionally require approvals and\ncontrolled maintenance windows.<\/p>\n<p>The US <strong>National Institute of Standards and Technology\n(NIST)<\/strong> describes in its IoT security baseline, among other\nthings, device identification, configuration, data protection, logical\naccess restrictions, updates and the observation of cybersecurity\nstates. This baseline is a starting point, <strong>not an automatic\ncertification or a universal legal requirement<\/strong>. <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8259/a/final\">Source: NIST IR 8259A,\n2020, checked 04.11.2026<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>Data protection becomes relevant above all when devices collect\npersonal information, such as presence, movement patterns, voices or\nimagery. Data volume can often be reduced: aggregate readings locally\nfirst, transmit only necessary events and limit retention periods.\n<strong>Not every temperature signal is personal data<\/strong>, but\ncombined with a room, time and occupants, even an apparently harmless\nseries of sensor readings may permit inferences. Specific legal duties\ndepend on the application and jurisdiction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical architecture I: the local media archive and its\nenvironmental sensor<\/h2>\n<p>A private media archive stores images, data carriers and possibly\nolder electronics. Monitoring temperature in an equipment cabinet\nrequires neither an AI model nor necessarily a cloud provider. A\nmicrocontroller with a temperature sensor can measure locally and\nactivate a warning light or buzzer.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Temperature sensor\n       |\n       v\nMicrocontroller ----&gt; Status LED / buzzer\n       |\n       +----&gt; Optional local log\n       |\n       +----&gt; Optional PC: display readings<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Why this architecture?<\/strong> The microcontroller can run\nindependently of the larger computer. It can also report when the\narchive PC is switched off. <strong>When is it unnecessary?<\/strong> If\nthe archive consists exclusively of digital files and no physical risk\nis to be monitored, the additional sensor solves no relevant\nproblem.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitations:<\/strong> A sensor in the warm airflow may not\nmeasure the temperature of all stored equipment. A dusty fan, absent\npower supply or broken cable remains possible. Safety-critical fire\nprotection would require certified systems designed for that purpose.\nThe home-built measurement sensor is <strong>not a substitute for smoke\ndetectors or safety systems<\/strong>. Maintenance includes battery\ncondition, sensor placement and occasional plausibility checks. With\npurely local data collection, data protection concerns are comparatively\nsmall; remote access increases them.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical architecture II: a networked building sensor<\/h2>\n<p>Temperature and humidity are to be monitored in several rooms of a\nlarger building. One sensor device per room would be appropriate where\ncables are impractical and measurements are to continue autonomously for\nlong periods. The devices do not send continuously, but for example\ntransmit periodically aggregated values to a gateway.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Room A: Sensor + MCU --+\nRoom B: Sensor + MCU --+--&gt; Local gateway\nRoom C: Sensor + MCU --+          |\n                                  v\n                           Database / analysis\n                                  |\n                                  v\n                           Management view<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Why not simply put everything in the cloud?<\/strong> A local\ngateway permits buffering when the Internet connection fails and\nseparates internal devices from publicly accessible interfaces. At the\nsame time, the gateway creates a new potential point of failure: its\nstorage, backups and maintenance must be planned. The entire system\nshould timestamp measurements, retain their traceable origins and\ndisplay gaps as gaps rather than inventing undelivered values.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operation and costs:<\/strong> Costs arise from devices,\nbattery replacement, installation, networking and possibly remote\nmaintenance. These operating costs may exceed the purchase price of a\nmicrocontroller. Data quality depends on the calibration and placement\nof the sensors. Access restrictions and a deletion policy are required\nfor presence detection or room data histories that can be linked to\nindividuals.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Useful extension:<\/strong> A local threshold alarm can work\nindependently, while long-term analysis only takes place on the server.\n<strong>Unnecessary extension:<\/strong> A large AI cluster for\npredicting simple threshold violations would often be oversized.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical architecture III: a mobile robot<\/h2>\n<p>A mobile robot combines almost all the topics of the lesson. It needs\nsensors, local motor control, a power supply, navigation and perhaps\nhigher-level AI. The tasks should be clearly separated:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Camera / LiDAR / task description\n                 |\n                 v\n       Navigation / task planning\n                 |\n                 v\n       Desired motion and limits\n                 |\n                 v\n       Local real-time control &lt;---- Encoder / IMU\n                 |\n                 v\n       Motor drivers and motors\n                 |\n                 v\n               Motion\n\nIndependent protective functions monitor the motion.<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An <strong>Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU)<\/strong> measures motion\nquantities such as acceleration and angular velocity.\n<strong>LiDAR<\/strong> (<em>Light Detection and Ranging<\/em>) measures\ndistances using light. Both help with orientation, but do not replace\nsafe motion planning. A more powerful computer can create maps; a local\nmicrocontroller handles the control tasks closer to the motor.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Why this division?<\/strong> Navigation may require more\ncomputing time than an internal motor control loop. A language model\ncould translate the task “Drive to the shelf” into a planned\ninstruction; however, it should not determine motor current values\ndirectly from free text. If the network fails, a suitable local\noperating state must remain available.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Risks:<\/strong> Measurement errors, blocked wheels,\nunpredictable obstacles, mechanical inertia and battery problems. A\nsimulation is useful, but does not replace testing on the actual device.\n<strong>Costs:<\/strong> Mechanics, safe sensor systems, tests and\nmaintenance can far exceed the price of the microcontroller. Applicable\nsafety standards and independent protective systems are also decisive\nfor real mobile machinery. The example remains an architectural\nconsideration, not a construction guide.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which approach is economically and technically suitable,\nand when?<\/h2>\n<p>The decision can start with four questions: <strong>Must the system\nrespond physically? How quickly? How much energy is available? What\nhappens in the event of failure?<\/strong> The answer prevents an\nexpensive mini PC being used where a low-power controller is enough, or\nconversely a chip with insufficient capacity being expected to perform\ncomplex image processing.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Application<\/th>\n<th>Often a sensible starting point<\/th>\n<th>What might justify additional complexity<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A single button with LED<\/td>\n<td>Microcontroller without an operating system<\/td>\n<td>Several time-critical tasks or remote administration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Periodic environmental sensor<\/td>\n<td>MCU with sleep mode, local buffering<\/td>\n<td>Networking many devices, long-term analysis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small networked installation<\/td>\n<td>MCU plus gateway<\/td>\n<td>Shared administration, security updates<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video analysis on a machine<\/td>\n<td>Powerful edge computer plus local control<\/td>\n<td>High image throughput, complex ML models<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Robot with navigation<\/td>\n<td>Separate planning, feedback control and safety functions<\/td>\n<td>Many sensors, demanding environments<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>It helps to consider <strong>purchase costs<\/strong>,\n<strong>development effort<\/strong> and <strong>life-cycle\ncosts<\/strong> separately. The cheapest controller need not be the\ncheapest overall solution if a reliable driver is missing or continuous\nmaintenance is required. Conversely, a complex operating system creates\nnew attack surfaces and update effort. Availability of spare parts,\ndocumentation and troubleshooting also belong to the long-term\ndecision.<\/p>\n<p>The distinction between “connected” and “dependent” is central: a\nsensor may be connected to a dashboard, but an important local\nprotective function must not depend on the dashboard being accessible.\nThis is the same architectural principle as separating\ntransaction-critical database functions from downstream analytics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The mathematics behind embedded devices<\/h2>\n<p>M9-L3 uses mathematics not as an end in itself, but to describe the\nreal world. <strong>Functions<\/strong> map measured voltages to physical\nquantities. <strong>Ratios and calculations with units<\/strong> help\nwith ADC resolution and battery life. <strong>Means<\/strong> reduce\nrandom measurement noise under suitable assumptions. <strong>Rates of\nchange<\/strong> describe how quickly a temperature rises or a motor\naccelerates. <strong>Vectors<\/strong> are important in spatial sensing\nand robotics.<\/p>\n<p>In advanced modules, rates of change lead to <strong>differential\nequations<\/strong> for dynamic systems. Control engineering describes\nhow a system changes over time and which interventions maintain a stable\nstate. <strong>Probability<\/strong> becomes important when sensors are\nnoisy or several estimates contradict one another. M9-L3 is therefore a\nnatural bridge to additional Master-level study of advanced robotics,\ncontrol and sensor data fusion.<\/p>\n<p>The precise comparison of subject matter and versions with the\nseparate mathematics course remains outstanding. These are therefore\n<strong>conceptually justified topic connections<\/strong>, not confirmed\nmutual lesson recommendations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>An embedded system connects computer science to concrete properties\nof a machine or its environment. A microcontroller executes small,\nclearly bounded programs and uses hardware blocks for inputs and\noutputs. Sensors turn physical processes into measurements; drivers and\ninterfaces transport these values. Actuators turn decisions into real\neffects.<\/p>\n<p>The most important technical distinctions are: <strong>digital is not\nanalogue<\/strong>, <strong>resolution is not accuracy<\/strong>,\n<strong>an interrupt is not a real-time guarantee<\/strong>,\n<strong>open-loop control is not closed-loop control<\/strong>,\n<strong>cloud connectivity is not a safety function<\/strong> and\n<strong>automatic restart is not proof of safety<\/strong>. A good design\nrespects these limits and starts with actual requirements, not with the\nmaximum possible number of components.<\/p>\n<p>The examples have shown that simple local technology is often the\nmost reliable starting point. Networking, operating systems and AI are\nsensible extensions when they solve a specific task better and their\nmaintenance and security implications can be managed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further computer science:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L2<\/a> (computer architecture), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a> (networks), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L3<\/a> (concurrency), <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L3<\/a> (security) and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L7<\/a> (robotics). <strong>Next\nBachelor elective lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L1 – Networks and practical IT\nsecurity<\/a>. <strong>Master bridge:<\/strong> Robotics, control\nengineering and sensor data fusion; so far only planned.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S22\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Position within the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This is an <strong>optional Bachelor elective lesson<\/strong>, not an\nadditional main-course lesson. The conceptual bridge leads, among other\nplaces, to <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4 in the Master\ncore<\/a>. Further routes: <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course-wide reading paths<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course index<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S23\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Subject-matter comparison on 04.11.2026.<\/strong> These are\ntechnical reference sources, not construction guides or product\npurchasing recommendations. The examples and numerical calculations in\nthis lesson are independently formulated teaching models.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Raspberry Pi: <a\nhref=\"https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/rp2040/specifications/\">RP2040\nSpecifications<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> and <a\nhref=\"https://github.com/raspberrypi/documentation/blob/master/documentation/asciidoc/microcontrollers/microcontroller-chips/rp2040.adoc\">RP2040\nhardware description<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – processor, memory, integrated peripherals and\nexternal flash.<\/li>\n<li>Arm: <a\nhref=\"https://arm-software.github.io/CMSIS_6/main/Core/group__NVIC__gr.html\">CMSIS-Core\n– Interrupts and Exceptions (NVIC)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – interrupt architecture and\nexceptions.<\/li>\n<li>FreeRTOS: <a\nhref=\"https://github.freertos.org/Documentation/02-Kernel/02-Kernel-features/01-Tasks-and-co-routines/04-Task-scheduling\">Task\nScheduling<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – scheduling, priorities, pre-emption and multicore\nconfigurations.<\/li>\n<li>Zephyr Project: <a\nhref=\"https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/hardware/peripherals/index.html\">Peripherals<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/hardware/peripherals/gpio.html\">GPIO<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/hardware/peripherals/adc.html\">ADC<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/hardware/peripherals/i2c.html\">I²C<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\nand <a href=\"https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/doxygen/html/\">API\ndocumentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – hardware abstraction and buses.<\/li>\n<li>NIST: <a href=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8259/a/final\">NIST IR\n8259A – IoT Device Cybersecurity Capability Core Baseline<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> and <a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/itl/applied-cybersecurity/nist-cybersecurity-iot-program/nistir-8259-series\">NIST\nIR 8259 Rev. 1 – Foundational Cybersecurity Activities for IoT Product\nManufacturers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – technical device security and long-term manufacturer\nresponsibility.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/article>"};
